\par\par \b \uc2 *\uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 : State  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\uc2  Amplify Operating Environment Risks for Hungarian Banks\b0\par\par\uc2 2,009 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2022\par 12:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 12 Dec 2022 06:15 ET  Press Release: \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 : State \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  Amplify Operating Environment Risks for Hungarian Banks\par \par \uc2 The following is a press release from \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 -Warsaw/London-12 December 2022: The most recent state \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Hungarian banking sector appear financially manageable but increase the risk of further policy \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  (including extending current measures), \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  says. This amplifies the already-high uncertainty in the operating environment for Hungarian banks and could weigh on creditor confidence. The \uc2 Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest estimate is that the domestic banking sector will absorb an additional regulatory burden of about HUF500 billion in 2022, mainly from the windfall tax (about HUF226 billion), an interest rate cap scheme (about HUF195 billion) and the increased financial transaction tax (FTT; about HUF40 billion). This is close to the sector's net profit in 2021 (HUF555 billion) and comes on top of the existing regular bank levy and FTT. Net interest income (NII) generation rose by about 42% yoy in 1H22 as policy rates increased, but inflation has increased costs and loan impairment charges are likely to rise given the worsened economic outlook. As a result, we believe that the sector's 2022 return on average assets (ROAA) could drop significantly from last year's 1.0%. We expect ROAA to recover in 2023, but potential further \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are a downside risk.\par \par \uc2 The windfall tax, binding in 2022 and 2023, was designed to collect banks' "extra" profits from a significant increase in policy rates, but is based on revenues rather than profits. Higher NII in 2022 was largely generated on loans and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits, although interest rate increases on the former were constrained by the regulatory caps and substantial fixed-rate lending. The loan interest rate cap scheme introduced in late 2021 to shield borrowers from rising borrowing costs has been extended several times in both scope and duration (currently until end-1H23). We think another extension is likely given that inflation and interest rates are likely to remain elevated until end-2023, which would mean additional loss of revenue for banks, though it would further mitigate asset-quality pressures. The scheme currently caps the reference rates for eligible mortgage loans and variable-rate SME loans at their end-October 2021 (about 2%) and end-June 2022 (7.8%) levels, respectively, considerably lower than the current three-month BUBOR rate (about 16%).\par \par \uc2 Broad-based borrower support measures imposed by the authorities, such as interest rate caps and the long-lasting loan repayment moratorium (which covered about 55% of household and about 44% of corporate loans at its peak in 2020), can support asset quality in the short term, but may increase moral hazard. This happens at a time when the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been strongly supporting credit growth through subsidised and state-guaranteed lending schemes for households and SMEs. This is likely to continue, although on a smaller scale given the government's fiscal constraints and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's focus on fighting inflation. According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the subsidised credit schemes accounted for about 46% of outstanding SME loans and about 40% of new household loan disbursements in 2Q22. Increased state \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionism\b0 \highlight\uc2  could negatively affect our view of Hungarian banks' operating environment and their performance prospects, and therefore could affect banks' Viability Ratings. Moreover, the record of state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has negatively affected our view of the potential extraordinary support available to rated Hungarian banks from their foreign parents. Since 2014, \uc2 Fitch\uc2  has capped Hungarian banks' Long-Term Issuer Default Ratings (IDRs) at one notch above the Hungarian sovereign rating (BBB/Stable) to reflect the country risks they face, in particular the risk of policy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , beyond that already captured by \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Country Ceiling of 'A'. The IDRs of two Hungarian banks rated by \uc2 Fitch\uc2  are constrained by the cap and are sensitive to changes in the sovereign IDR. Contacts: Agata Gryglewicz Director, Financial Institutions -- Banks +48 22 103 3013 \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  Ireland Ltd. Sp\u243\'00\'F3 ka z ograniczon odpowiedzialno\u347\'01\'5Bci Oddzia w Polsce Krolewska 16, 00-103 Warsaw, Poland Monika Panasiuk Associate Director, Financial Institutions -- Banks +48 22 103 3028 Mark Brown Senior Director, Fitch Wire +44 20 3530 1588\par \par \uc2 Media Relations: Peter Fitzpatrick, London, Tel: +44 20 3530 1103, Email: peter.fitzpatrick@thefitchgroup.com\par \par \uc2 The above article originally appeared as a post on the Fitch Wire credit market commentary page. The original article can be accessed at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\uc2 . All opinions expressed are those of \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All \uc2 Fitch