\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS- \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  ease as cenbanks attempt to halt losses\b0\par\par\uc2 812 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 10:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Anita Komuves\par     BUDAPEST, March 3 (Reuters) - Central European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par eased on Thursday, with the Polish zloty and the Hungarian\par forint failing to significantly benefit from steps taken by both\par the countries' central banks to help shore up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the region plunged since last week in a\par selloff sparked by Russia's invasion of Ukraine.\par     The Hungarian forint was down 0.33% on the day and\par trading at 379.50 per euro on Thursday, paring some\par early-morning losses after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  raised its one-week\par deposit rate by 75 basis points to 5.35%, stepping up the pace\par of rate rises from last week.  \par     The bank uses the one-week deposit rate to tackle short-term\par market volatility. On Wednesday, the forint fell to a record low\par at 382.61 versus the euro, taking its losses to nearly 6% within\par a week since the start of the invasion.\par     "This rate hike will be enough to scare speculators away but\par not enough to lead to significant strenghtening. That won't\par happen before news of a solution to the war arrive," a\par Budapest-based \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader said.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said on Tuesday it was ready to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "at any moment" to ensure the stability of local\par financial markets.\par     The Polish zloty slid 0.59% and was trading at\par 4.7722 versus the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recouped some earlier losses on Wednesday and\par firmed back from levels around 4.8 after the the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par and the finance ministry moved to support a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that had\par sunk to a 13-year low.\par     "The disproportionate depreciation pressure on the Polish\par zloty and Hungarian forint are consistent with the dovish\par monetary stances which these central banks have continued to\par maintain," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  wrote.\par     "Both central banks are running deep negative real interest\par rates ... which 'expose' their exchange rates, more than others,\par to the external volatility." \par     Elsewhere, the Czech crown was 0.88% lower and\par trading at 25.741 per euro while the Romanian leu slid\par 0.09% to 4.9485 to the euro.\par     Most stocks in the region gained, recouping some losses from\par the past week. Budapest led gains as it added 3.4% while\par Prague was 2.5% up. Bucharest was 2.23% higher\par while Warsaw bucked the trend and slid 0.68%.\par     Even so, regional stock markets remained deeply in the red\par for the year, led by an 18.5% plunge in Budapest stocks.\par     \par                   CEE      SNAPSH   AT                   \par                   MARKETS  OT      0956            \par                                    CET             \par                            CURREN                        \par                            CIES                    \par                            Latest  Previo  Daily   Change\par                                    us              \par                        