\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS-Forint hits record low despite c.bank vow\b0\par\par\uc2 752 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 11:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Alan Charlish\par     WARSAW, March 2 (Reuters) - The Hungarian forint\par sank to a record low against the euro on Wednesday and the\par Polish zloty hit its weakest since 2009 as central\par banks said they would act to contain the impact on markets of\par the conflict in Ukraine.\par     Russia's invasion of its neighbour and the resulting raft of\par sanctions unleashed on Moscow have sent investors scurrying for\par safety, leading them to drop assets seen as less secure.\par     "There is a war at our borders," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Peter Virovacz\f28 \uc2 \f2 , senior\par economist at ING in Budapest. "The region is facing a flight to\par safety that means all of the Hungarian, Czech and Polish assets\par are under pressure."\par     At 1012 GMT, the Hungarian forint was 1.07% weaker against\par the euro at 381.15 and the zloty was 1.42% softer at 4.815.\par     The Czech crown slid 0.57% to 25.58, while the\par Romanian leu was little changed at 4.949.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said on Tuesday it was ready to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "at any moment" to ensure the stability of local\par financial markets, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Poland's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold a certain\par amount of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for zlotys after saying it was\par ready to react to excessive moves in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \par     However, this looked to have had little effect in halting\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ' slide.\par     "In our opinion, another \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is possible,"\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Millennium\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts in Warsaw said in a note. "It would\par happen if the zloty depreciation - like yesterday - proceeded\par too quickly (compared to the region), with no new important\par information boosting risk aversion."\par     Stocks were mostly lower, but Warsaw's WIG 20 index\par was 2.96% higher, boosted by energy companies.\par     Coking coal producer \f28 \uc2 \f2 JSW\f28 \uc2 \f2  was the biggest gainer for\par the third day in a row, rising 19.5% on the back of potential\par sanctions against coal imported from Russia.\par     On Tuesday, Polish Prime Minister Mateusz Morawiecki said\par Poland wanted the strongest possible sanctions to be introduced\par against Russia, including a ban on the import of coal.\par \par     \par     \par     \par                    CEE      SNAPSHO   AT                      \par                    MARKETS  T        1112              \par                                      CET               \par                                                               \par                    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENC\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                     \par                    IES                                 \par                             Latest   Previou  Daily    Change\par                                      s                 \par                             bid      close    change   in 2022\par  EURCZK  Czech     <EURCZK  25.5800  25.4330   -0.57%   -2.77%\par  =       crown     =>                                  \par  EURHUF  Hungary   <EURHUF  381.150  377.085   -1.07%   -3.09%\par  =       forint    =>             0        0           \par  EURPLN  Polish    <EURPLN   4.8150   4.7469   -1.42%   -4.65%\par  =       zloty     =>                                  