\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-\uc2 Hungary  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  ready to act 'at any moment' to shore up markets\b0\par\par\uc2 592 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2022\par 19:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Forint reined in from record lows by cenbank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Still down sharply since start of Russian invasion of Ukraine\par \par \uc2 * Cenbank must continue tightening cycle -deputy governor\par \par \uc2 * Inflation developments harming growth prospects -deputy governor (Adds comments from deputy governor, updates market reaction)\par \par \uc2 By Gergely Szakacs\par \par \uc2 BUDAPEST, March 1 (Reuters) - The \uc2 National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBH) is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  "at any moment" with all tools at its disposal to ensure the stability of local financial markets, it said in a reply to Reuters questions on Tuesday, reining in the forint from record lows.\par \par \uc2 The NBH raised its base rate by 50 basis points to 3.4% last week, as expected, after consumer prices grew at their fastest rate in almost 15 years in January and mounting Ukraine-Russia tensions fuelled market uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 The NBH said it was closely monitoring the situation, adding that current market moves were not justified by economic fundamentals after the forint fell as much as 2% on the day to record lows versus the euro.\par \par \uc2 "It is the NBH's clear intention to prevent the heightened risks stemming from geopolitical circumstances from threatening Hungary's price and financial stability," it said.\par \par \uc2 "Financial market moves are not justified by fundamentals, however, they add to upside risks for inflation."\par \par \uc2 Deputy Governor \uc2 Barnabas Virag\uc2  told private broadcaster Info Radio in an interview that the NBH must continue its monetary tightening cycle in a predictable way, but also stand ready to act in a flexible manner, if needed.\par \par \uc2 "The situation will be more difficult throughout the year because of the war," Virag said. "We must prepare for an increase in inflation risks and a deterioration of the economic growth outlook."\par \par \uc2 Virag said given the high degree of uncertainty over the outcome of the military conflict, it was not possible to give predictable economic forecasts, adding that the road back to the bank's 3% inflation target will be a long one.\par \par \uc2 Earlier on Tuesday, economists at \uc2 Raiffeisen Bank\uc2  sharply raised their inflation forecasts for Hungary to 9% this year and 7% for 2023, with the average rate for two years projected at the highest for the entire Central European region.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  is closely monitoring the situation and stands ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any moment," it said. "If necessary, the NBH will use all tools at its disposal to ensure the stability of local financial markets."\par \par \uc2 The war over the eastern border in neighbouring Ukraine, home to a large ethnic Hungarian community, has hammered Hungarian financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The forint has fallen some 4% versus the euro since Russia invaded Ukraine last week, sinking to record lows at 379 per euro on Tuesday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pared some of its losses after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remarks, but was still down more than 1% on the day, extending its losses for the year.\par \par \uc2 Shares in central Europe's largest independent lender, \uc2 OTP Bank\uc2 , plunged 21.5% on Tuesday, hitting their lowest level since November 2020. The bank's shares have lost about a third of their value since Russia invaded Ukraine last week.\par \par \uc2 The NBH's next regular policy meeting is due on March 22. It will hold a non-rate setting meeting next Tuesday, while the weekly tender of its one-week deposit facility, which it uses to tackle short-term market volatility, is due on Thursday. (Reporting by Gergely Szakacs and Anita Komuves in Budapest Editing by Alex Richardson and Matthew Lewis) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-3-1T19:22:47.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220301ei3103sq9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
