\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS-Forint rebounds further from record low after 1-week depo rate rise\b0\par\par\uc2 787 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2021\par 11:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Jason Hovet and Anita Komuves\par     PRAGUE/BUDAPEST, Nov 25 (Reuters) - The forint gained for\par second day on Thursday and bounced further off an all-time low\par to lead gains in central Europe, after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hungary's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par raised its one-week deposit rate by 40 basis points to 2.9% as\par it fights rising inflation risks.\par     The increase in the depo rate was the second in a week,\par coming after the bank raised its main base rate to 2.1% this\par month and said it was ready to lift the rate on one-week\par deposits above the base rate, which traders and analysts have\par said could support the forint.\par     By 0949 GMT, the forint had risen 0.55% on the day\par to 366.7 to the euro, off a session high near 368.\par     "This (depo rate rise) will be enough to put out the fire,\par because the forint has been really singled out lately, but the\par international environment has to change really for the forint to\par be able to significantly firm," a trader said.\par     The forint touched an all-time low of 372 to the euro on\par Tuesday as central Europe's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  come under pressure due\par to strong U.S. dollar along with a surge in COVID-19 cases\par around Europe that has investors worried about economic impacts.\par     Hungary reported a record daily tally in new infections on\par Wednesday, following the same trend in the Czech Republic and\par Slovakia whose governments have already tightened restrictions\par to stem the surge. Slovakia entered a two-week lockdown on\par Thursday.\par     Elsewhere, the Polish zloty gained 0.2% and the\par Czech crown was up 0.1%.\par     Central banks around the region have launched policy\par tightening in reaction to inflation spikes that are due to\par global factors amid supply chain problems and rising energy\par costs, as well as tight labour markets in central Europe.\par     Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Adam Glapinski\f28 \uc2 \f2  told Interia\par website on Wednesday that recent rate hikes should help the\par zloty, which has touched 12-year lows.\par     "Should the strengthening of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  be short-lived,\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could step-in and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the market,"\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Erste Group Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  said.\par     \par  ** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's forint since 2008: \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3xoDmH6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3xoDmH6}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     \par     \par     \par                    CEE      SNAPSHO   AT                      \par                    MARKETS  T        1049              \par                                      CET               \par                                                               \par                    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENC\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                     \par                    IES                                 \par                             Latest   Previou  Daily    Change\par                                      s                 \par                             bid      close    change   in 2021\par  EURCZK  Czech     <EURCZK  25.4670  25.4975   +0.12%   +2.99%\par  =       crown     =>                                  \par  EURHUF  Hungary   <EURHUF  366.700  368.710   +0.55% 