\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 : CEE QE Supports Coronavirus Response but Brings Macro Risks\b0\par\par\uc2 1,863 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2020\par \uc2 Fitch Rating / Non Rating Action Commentary\par \uc2 FITRA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 by Fitch Ratings Inc., Fitch Ratings Ltd. and its subsidiaries. \par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 -London-03 June 2020: Quantitative easing (QE) in Central and Eastern Europe (CEE) has aided coronavirus crisis responses, but could bring macroeconomic risks if used excessively, says \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 . Risks would be more pronounced if QE undermined perceptions of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  independence and economic policy framework credibility.\par \par \uc2 Croatia, Poland and Romania have all debuted QE since the pandemic began, while the \uc2 Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MNB) restarted government bond buying. Not all monetary authorities have outlined specific goals, but purchases so far have mostly been small and appear focused on smoothing volatility in domestic government debt markets and lowering longer-dated bond yields. The \uc2 National Bank of Poland\uc2  (NBP) has been more active, building a portfolio equivalent to 3.2% of GDP across the yield curve.\par \par \uc2 All four countries face large financing needs in 2020. Policy rate cuts in Romania and Poland have underpinned lower funding costs - rates in Hungary were already at the zero lower bound (ZLB) - but QE has likely contributed. Indeed, QE purchases may have been calibrated with reference to funding conditions, which have been relatively supportive. Non-resident outflows from core CEE markets during March were proportionately smaller than from other EM regions, and April saw inflows. The MNB initially announced HUF1,000 billion of purchases between May and July, but only bought around HUF150 billion last month, with weekly purchases falling as yields declined.\par \par \uc2 Lower financing costs are supportive of sovereign ratings in the short-term, as noted in our recent report\par \par \uc2 What Investors Want to Know: \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Purchases of Government Bonds. However, we also highlighted potential medium-term risks from QE, which are often greater in emerging markets (EMs).\par \par \uc2 By increasing the money supply, unsterilised QE can put pressure on exchange rates. Croatia and Romania have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightened monetary conditions in April, suggesting that awareness of this risk may be limiting the size of their QE programmes. Meanwhile the \uc2 Czech National Bank (CNB) \uc2 cited potential exchange rate destabilisation as a reason not to adopt QE, although the CNB can buy Czech government bonds following recent legislative changes.\par \par \uc2 The exchange rate impact of crowding non-residents out of local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt is probably lower, given high local ownership of government debt (75%-80% of the total). The desire to introduce new local demand given high domestic bank holdings may have partly motivated QE.\par \par \uc2 QE also brings inflation risks through the money supply and exchange rate channels. QE has a mixed record at pushing inflation higher, but research suggests it raised inflation in developed markets (DMs) following the global financial crisis relative to the counterfactual of no QE. The opening of large negative output gaps suggests inflationary pressures will be muted for some time in CEE, but with QE untested in the region, the transmission to prices may be faster than in DMs.\par \par \uc2 None of the CEE central banks pursuing QE has a mandate for primary market purchases of government debt, which would be tantamount to financing the fiscal deficit, potentially damaging perceptions of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  independence. However, some NBP \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  resemble direct monetary financing.\par \par \uc2 More broadly, with interest rates likely to stay very low, and as it helps reduce funding costs, QE is likely to become a permanent part of the authorities' policy toolkit in CEE. High funding needs could result in greater political pressure to reduce the threshold for its future use.\par \par \uc2 Contact:\par \par \uc2 Paul Gamble\par \par \uc2 Senior Director, Sovereigns\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1623\par \par \uc2 Fitch Ratings Limited\par \par \uc2 30 North Colonnade\par \par \uc2 London E14 5GN\par \par \uc2 Brian Coulton\par \par \uc2 Chief Economist\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1140\par \par \uc2 Mark Brown\par \par \uc2 Senior Director, Fitch Wire\par \par \uc2 +44 20 3530 1588\par \par \uc2 Media Relations: Peter Fitzpatrick, London, Tel: +44 20 3530 1103, Email: peter.fitzpatrick@thefitchgroup.com\par \par \uc2 The above article originally appeared as a post on the Fitch Wire credit market commentary page. The original article can be accessed at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.fitchratings.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.fitchratings.com}}}\uc2 . All opinions expressed are those of \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ALL FITCH CREDIT RATINGS ARE SUBJECT TO CERTAIN LIMITATIONS AND DISCLAIMERS. PLEASE READ THESE LIMITATIONS AND DISCLAIMERS BY FOLLOWING THIS LINK: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://WWW.FITCHRATINGS.COM/UNDERSTANDINGCREDITRATINGS"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://WWW.FITCHRATINGS.COM/UNDERSTANDINGCREDITRATINGS}}}\uc2 . IN ADDITION, RATING DEFINITIONS AND THE TERMS OF USE OF SUCH RATINGS ARE AVAILABLE ON THE AGENCY'S PUBLIC WEB SITE AT WWW.FITCHRATINGS.COM. PUBLISHED RATINGS, CRITERIA, AND METHODOLOGIES ARE AVAILABLE FROM THIS SITE AT ALL TIMES. FITCH'S CODE OF CONDUCT, CONFIDENTIALITY, CONFLICTS OF INTEREST, AFFILIATE FIREWALL, COMP