\par\par \b \uc2 NBH Unlikely to Offer Response to Rising Bubor, HUF to Fall: ING -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,529 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2018\par 13:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1146 GMT - The \uc2 National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only likely to verbally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the fall of the Hungarian forint, says ING, adding it doesn't "expect no actual NBH response" to the fact that EUR/HUF has risen to 317 from 310 in a month. The three-month Budapest interbank offered rate, or Bubor, has also shot up to 0.1% from 0.02% just a few weeks ago and the 10-year Hungarian forint interest rate swap has risen to 255 basis points on Tuesday, the highest level in a year. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to remain in wait-and-see mode" at the interest rate decision due 1200 GMT, ING says. A lack of NBH response may push EUR/HUF toward the 319 level again, ING adds. EUR/HUF is last down 0.2% at 317.42. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1134 GMT - The three-month Budapest interbank offered rate, or Bubor, has shot up to 0.10% from 0.02% a few weeks ago, and the 10-year Hungarian forint interest rate swap has risen to 255 basis points, the highest level in a year, says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  . This raises questions over whether the \uc2 National Bank of Hungary\uc2  will "double down on its dovish rhetoric and unconventional means to keep both front and long-end yields suppressed," SocGen says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which signaled before that it wants to anchor long-term funding costs and keep the yield curve flat, announces its rate decision at 1200 GMT. EUR/HUF falls 0.2% to 317.36. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 1120 GMT - The final steps toward a new antiestablishment Italian government presents challenges for the country's economic and sovereign outlook, and may impede progress on the European reform agenda, according to Scope Ratings. The Italian economy holds structural weaknesses tied to its low growth, high public-deficit of 132% of gross domestic product, alongside institutional bottlenecks, and "the composition and inexperience of this probable new government cast doubt on the country's future commitment and ability to resolves such challenges," say analysts Dennis Shen and Giacomo Barisone. The implementation of even some of the suggested measures, probable inaction on other areas of needed reform, and extended political uncertainty cloud the path ahead for Italy's 'A-/Stable' sovereign rating, Scope Ratings' analysts say. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 1054 GMT - The difficult outlook for gold remains even as falls in the dollar have halted the descent of the metal's price, says spread-betting company IG. "Gold's decline has stalled, which has given precious metal miners a breathing space, but overall the difficult outlook for the commodity remains," says IG's Chris Beauchamp. "It's not clear whether the recent weakness in the dollar marks a top in the rally of the past few months, but with the market still broadly short USD, it's easy to imagine the greenback perking up in due course, even if tomorrow's Fed minutes are not overly hawkish. Ahead of the open, we expect the Dow to start at 25,056, up 43 points from Monday's close." (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1052 GMT - Italian government bonds pare some of Monday's losses but rising five-year credit default swap prices indicate that investors remain concerned over the new government's fiscal plans. "Investors appear concerned over motivations as much as plans," say \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  analysts. While a modification of the fiscal plans may appease President \uc2 Sergio Mattarella\uc2  , "it may require more to ease investor concerns, where a Greek-like crisis is feared, and worse given the size of Italy's economy and market," \uc2 BBH\uc2  analysts say. The 10-year Italian BTP yield is trading 6.2 basis points lower at 2.318% at 1044 GMT, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . The five-year Italian CDS is indicated at 140.85 bps, up from 124.11 bps at the end of last week, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 . (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 1018 GMT - For now, EUR/USD is trading sideways from 1.1750, last up 0.3% at 1.1824 as the spread between 10-year Italian and \uc2 German government\uc2  bonds is narrowing, but if the BTP-Bund spread were to widen to 213 basis points, "then EUR/USD could cascade down to 1.15," according to \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  . "For now though, the rot in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to have been stopped." Whether "a tactical bounce follows may boil down to the eurozone flash" PMIs and the Federal Open Market Committee minutes on Wednesday, SocGen says. According to a WSJ poll, manufacturing \uc2 PMI\uc2  likely has stayed flat at 56 and services \uc2 PMI\uc2  likely has fallen to 54.5 in May. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0937 GMT - Eight years ago today, on May 22, 2010, the programmer \uc2 Laszlo Hanyecz\uc2  bought two pizzas using 10,000 bitcoin. This is widely recognized as the first real-world transaction using bitcoin. If those pizzas were bought now, with bitcoin trading at $8,268, they would cost $82.7 million--a rather pricey lunch. Commemorating that day puts into perspective how much the cryptocurrency has gained in value since then, and highlights potential issues surrounding its purchasing power in the future. Many critics say, however, that bitcoin could never be a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because its value fluctuates too much. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0911 GMT - EUR/USD is likely to stay above the 1.1717 support level on Tuesday, according to ING. With EUR/USD continuing "to be primarily driven by Italian politics and spiking BTP-Bund spreads," EUR/USD is up by 0.1% at 1.1809 as the spread is shrinking, last at 173 basis points. Italian President \uc2 Sergio Mattarella\uc2  is due to meet with the speakers of the lower and upper house today, which \uc2 RBC\uc2  says "many have taken as a sign he will try push back on the League/5 Star plans and their proposed choice for Prime Minister," who is \uc2 Giuseppe Conte\uc2 , a law professor aligned with the Five-Star Movement. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0826 GMT - The dollar is reversing early gains versus the euro and now trades lower, with EUR/USD up 0.3% at 1.1827. USD/JPY is still flat at 111.02. Commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are also up against the dollar, with USD/CAD down 0.3% at 1.2754 and AUD/USD up 0.3% at 0.7604. "If the strength in risk-related \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  [such as the Australian dollar] proves sustainable, this might pave the way for a reversal in EUR/USD once the BTP-Bund spread starts narrowing again," says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  . EUR/USD has been falling since mid-April, from around 1.24. The spread between 10-year Italian and \uc2 German government\uc2  bonds falls from 185 to 173 basis points Tuesday morning.(olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0812 GMT - Keep an eye on the Swiss franc as a gauge of European risks, say analysts at BNY Mellon. The euro-franc rate "remains a key barometer of European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  concerns," chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Simon Derrick wrote in a note. Last year's French elections were a "key turning point" in terms of strengthening the euro, he noted. However, "pressures are building once again" in light of Italy's political developments, he noted, and the euro's 2% drop against the franc in recent days "is already challenging the broad trend up from the time of the French presidential election." Euro trading at 1.1766 francs early Tuesday, little changed from Monday. (brian.blackstone@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0735 GMT - U.K. gilt market makers prefer early to mid-July for the second syndicated transaction of the 2018-2019 financial year, the Debt Management Office says after Monday's consultation meetings with gilt market participants ahead of the announcement of the July-September gilt \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  calendar. Market makers' views were broadly split between those favoring launching a new index-linked gilt in the 25-year area--with 2041 and 2043 mentioned the most as preferred maturities--and those favoring reopening the 2048 index-linked gilt, the DMO says. Investors were divided between launching a new index-linked gilt maturing in the 20- to 25-year area and reopening the 2048 index-linked gilt. Both market makers and investors had isolated calls for other maturities. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0703 GMT - Spain's upcoming treasury bill auction Tuesday may attract more attention than usual following strong sell-off in Italian government bonds Monday that spilled over into Spanish, Portuguese and many other eurozone bonds. \uc2 Mizuho\uc2  rates strategists say that "given the stress on higher yielding markets yesterday, seeing these [Spanish treasury bill] auctions solidly bid could well generate a small rally" in Spanish government bonds. On Tuesday the Spanish treasury offers EUR1.5 billion to EUR2.5 billion in three- and nine-month treasury bills. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 22, 2018 07:46 ET (11:46 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180522ee5m001bk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
