\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Affirms Hungary At 'BBB-/A-3'; Outlook Positive\b0\par\par\uc2 3,252 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 2018\par 22:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 16 Feb 2018 16:05 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Hungary At 'BBB-/A-3'; Outlook Positive\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  : \par  \par Ratings: \par Foreign and Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : BBB-/Positive/A-3 \par  \par For further details see ratings list. \par  \par OVERVIEW \par  \par      -- Hungary is entering another year of strong, cyclical economic recovery, \par with real GDP growth projected at 3.5% in 2018. \par      -- Its current account has started to erode, but the external profile \par remains strong, and while bank lending remains slow, we see structural \par improvements in the banking system. \par      -- We are affirming our 'BBB-/A-3' sovereign credit ratings on Hungary. \par      -- The outlook remains positive. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par  \par On Feb. 16, 2018, S&P Global Ratings affirmed its 'BBB-/A-3' long- and \par short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on Hungary. The \par outlook remains positive. \par  \par We have withdrawn our 'BBB-' ratings on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of Hungary\f28 \uc2 \f2   (\f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar \par Nemzeti Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  ; MNB or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) at the bank's request. \par  \par OUTLOOK \par  \par The positive outlook primarily reflects the possibility that Hungary's \par strengthening economic metrics might support ongoing improvements in the \par banking sector, thereby reinforcing the monetary transmission channel. \par  \par We could raise the ratings in the next 12-18 months if recent improvements in \par the financial sector enhanced the authorities' ability to influence domestic \par monetary conditions. This would be signalled, for example, by a stronger \par recovery of bank lending alongside continued economic growth, accompanied by \par current account surpluses and contained fiscal deficits. \par  \par We could revise the outlook to stable if lending growth remained subdued and \par banks' nonperforming loans (NPLs) started increasing. In addition, we could \par take a negative rating action if Hungary's public finances weakened, external \par vulnerabilities increased, exchange rate policy became more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventionist\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , or \par its institutions weakened, for example in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's relationship with the EU , \par especially if we anticipated fiscal risk associated with such developments. \par  \par RATIONALE \par  \par Hungary's strong external profile and track record of fiscal restraint support \par the sovereign ratings. Relatively weak checks and balances between government \par branches, moderate long-term growth prospects, high public debt, and so far \par impaired monetary transmission mechanism are key constraints. \par  \par Institutional and Economic Profile: Strong cyclical growth amid a weaker \par institutional setting \par      -- Cyclical upswing benefits from external environment and government \par stimulus. \par      -- Structural challenges may contain longer-term growth. \par      -- Weakened checks and balances constrain the predictability of \par policymaking. \par  \par Like its regional peers, Hungary is undergoing a strong cyclical recovery. We \par expect real GDP growth will approach 3.5% in 2018, only modestly lower than in \par 2017. This is due to booming consumption, triggered by real wage and employment \par gains; fiscal stimulus and minimum wage hikes; substantial recovery in EU  \par structural and cohesion fund inflows; rising expenditure on housing subsidies; \par stronger private-sector balance sheets; and the ongoing recovery in the \par eurozone. In 2017, after a 10-year lapse, real consumption and investment in \par Hungary recovered to precrisis levels. \par  \par However, our growth projection for this year exceeds the country's long-term \par growth potential. Over the medium term, we expect Hungary's structural \par challenges will likely slow the pace of average GDP growth to just under 2.5% \par per year in 2019-2021. These include poor demographics, resulting partly from \par the government's reluctance to accept migrant workers; a large public sector; \par weaknesses in the business environment; low productivity; and an overheating \par labor market. \par  \par Moreover, given the prominence of EU  funds in Hungary's public investments, the \par country's growth outlook after 2020 will be exposed to risks of post-Brexit \par funding cuts and likely changes to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  's structural fund allocation \par criteria, not least because of the explicit disagreements between Hungary and \par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2   on some policy issues. \par  \par We think that some unconventional policies of the current government, aided by \par a favorable external environment, have helped reduce Hungary's external \par vulnerabilities and secure macroeconomic stability. We also acknowledge the \par government's ambitious reform agenda, aimed at boosting competitiveness and \par productivity. However, we also think institutional changes implemented since \par the early 2010s, which re-codified legal frameworks for Hungary's key \par institutions (for example, the MNB and constitutional court) have weakened the \par checks and balances between branches of government. In particular, the \par consequent concentration of power has expanded the influence and size of the \par public sector at the expense of governance, in our view. \par  \par Moreover, restrictive actions taken against the independent mass media and the \par clamp down on nongovernmental organizations have resulted in further political \par centralization, undermining the effectiveness of Hungary's institutions and the \par predictability of its policymaking. Three ongoing infringement procedures \par against Hungary could destabilize Hungary's relationship with the EU , \par potentially derailing EU  investment projects and eroding private-sector \par investor confidence. In our base-case scenario, we assume that such policies \par will continue to constrain Hungary's ability to implement structural reforms \par and effectively address periods of economic distress. At the same time, we \par believe significant financial sanctions or cuts in structural funds to Hungary \par for noncompliance with \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2   policy are less likely before 2020, when the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  's \par current financial framework ends. \par  \par Flexibility and Performance Profile: Signs of a solid lending growth recovery \par      -- Procyclical fiscal stance will unlikely allow government debt to drop \par below 60% of GDP in the medium term. \par      -- Surging domestic demand and higher energy prices will start consuming \par current account surpluses. \par      -- Stronger lending demand, historically low interest rates, gradually \par reducing NPLs, and several government-sponsored and -initiated schemes support \par credit growth recovery. \par  \par Despite the government's intentions to continue its fiscal stimulus via tax \par cuts and higher spending, we assume in our base case that fiscal slippage will \par be contained, resulting in general government deficits of 2.2% of GDP on \par average in 2018-2021. This is despite a slightly wider fiscal deficit in 2018 \par due to upcoming parliamentary elections, compared with 2% of GDP on average in \par 2016-2017, which was supported by asset sales and strong consumption-related \par tax receipts. The 2018 budget targets a deficit of 2.4% of GDP, excluding \par revenues from potential privatization receipts, which could therefore augment \par budgetary performance. \par  \par We forecast that general government debt, net of liquid assets, will reduce \par somewhat, but stay above 60% of GDP throughout our forecast horizon. At the \par same time, we note improvements in Hungary's central government debt profile. \par Concentrated policy efforts--particularly the authorities' self-financing \par program initiated in 2014--have yielded a reduction in Hungary's foreign \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -denominated debt to about one-fifth of total outstanding debt. \par Nonresidents' holdings of central government debt have also declined, reaching \par about 37% in 2017 compared with 40% in 2016, while resident commercial banks \par increased their exposure to the sovereign to over 20% of total banking system \par assets. Hungary has also benefitted from the decline in global interest rates \par and has been able to refinance its outstanding commercial debt with cheaper \par borrowings. This prompted a reduction in general government interest costs to \par about 6.6% of general government revenues in 2017 from its peak of nearly 10% \par in 2012. \par  \par Our fiscal forecast does not factor in any contingent risk, such as capital \par injections into development institutions, new state acquisitions, or spending \par on visible capital projects. If these developments crystalize, they could weigh \par on the government's fiscal and debt profiles. \par  \par Hungary's external financial profile--which has improved considerably since \par 2009--remains a credit strength. Successive current account surpluses since \par 2010, the repayment of a large portion of banks' external debt, the redemption \par of official loans contracted in relation to balance-of-payments assistance, and \par the government's substitution of external debt with domestic borrowings have \par all aided external deleveraging. The banking sector is now in a net external \par creditor position. \par  \par At the same time, we consider that higher global energy prices and surging \par domestic demand will reduce current account surpluses to below 1% of GDP in \par 2021 from an exceptionally strong 6% in 2016. However, this will likely be \par counterbalanced by other non-debt inflows, primarily EU  funds via the capital \par account, resulting in the continued reduction of our preferred external debt \par measure and narrow net external debt, which has more than halved since 2009. \par  \par We continue to believe that Hungary's monetary transmission channel remains \par hampered by a still sizable, although reducing, amount of legacy NPLs in \par commercial real estate and banks' residual dependence on foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par financing. However, these constraints--which have contained the MNB's monetary \par policy effectiveness for years--have started to subside, in our view. \par  \par Following a visible recovery of bank lending in 2017 (in absolute terms), when \par credit to the private sector increased by over 4%, we see a higher likelihood \par of sustainable lending growth, including to households. This reflects the \par impact of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's credit-boosting programs (such as the Market-Based \par Lending Scheme for small and midsize enterprises, and certified consumer \par friendly housing loans) and strengthening credit demand, notably thanks to \par historically low interest rates. For 2018-2021, we forecast positive credit \par growth (averaging 4.5%-5%) after a contraction in 2011-2016. We note, however, \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16 Feb 2018 16:06 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Affirms Hungary At -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par that the pace of credit growth remains below that of nominal GDP growth, and we \par forecast the share of domestic credit to non-financial private enterprises will \par remain below 40% of GDP. At the same time, supported by the recovery in real \par estate prices, the sale of NPLs to asset-management companies, and \par macroprudential measures (like higher capital requirements for banks with a \par high share of NPLs in the commercial real estate segment), the stock of NPLs is \par likely to be lower than 10% of systemwide loans in 2018-2019, well below the \par peak levels in 2013. The banking sector is likely to remain profitable and well \par capitalized in the medium term, due to the normalization of credit costs, \par continuing the trend of higher profits reported in 2016 and 2017, which stemmed \par from structural as well as one-time factors. This, in addition to improved \par regulation and supervision, led us to revise our Banking Industry Country Risk \par Assessment of the Hungarian banking sector to group '6' from group '7', on a \par scale from '1' to '10', with group '1' denoting the lowest risk banking \par industry (see \par "Articlehtml \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.capitaliq.com/CIQDotNet/CreditResearch/SPResearch.aspx?ArtObjectId=10221713&ArtRevId=1xt_artcl-mark-hyperlnkSeptember"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.capitaliq.com/CIQDotNet/CreditResearch/SPResearch.aspx?ArtObjectId=10221713&ArtRevId=1xt_artcl-mark-hyperlnkSeptember }}}\uc2 \f2  \par 19, 2017 06:48:51 AMBanking Industry Country Risk Assessment: Hungary," \par published Sept. 19, 2017, on RatingsDirect). \par  \par Strong private consumption and elevated wage growth have pushed price inflation \par up, with the consumer prices index hitting 2.4% in 2017 compared with 0.5% in \par 2016. This is still below the MNB's target, however, partly owing to stable \par administrative prices, recent tax cuts, and low inflation expectations. \par Nonetheless, in the medium term, tight labor markets, strong domestic demand, \par and rising energy costs, could lead to a surge in prices, bringing the consumer \par price index to 3% in 2018. \par  \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RATINGS SCORE SNAPSHOT \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA AND RESEARCH \par  \par Related Criteria \par      -- Criteria - Governments - Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology - \par December 18,2017 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments - May 18,2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Use Of CreditWatch And Outlooks - September 14,2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Long-Term And Short-Term \par Ratings - April 07,2017 \par  \par Related Research \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends 2018 - January 10, 2018 \par      -- Central And Eastern Europe And CIS Sovereign Rating Trends 2018 - \par January 10, 2018 \par      -- Sovereign Ratings History - February 07, 2018 \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators - December 14, 2017. An interactive version \par is available at  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.spratings.com/sri }}}\uc2 \f2  \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Outlook 2018: Brighter With Risk Of Turbulence \par - December 13, 2017 \par      -- Default, Transition, and Recovery: 2016 Annual Sovereign Default Study \par And Rating Transitions - April 03, 2017 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to make \par an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and explained the \par recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and critical issues \par in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and quantitative risk \par factors were considered and discussed, looking at track-record and forecasts. \par  \par The committee agreed that all other key rating factors were unchanged. \par  \par The chair ensured every voting member was given the opportunity to articulate \par his/her opinion. The chair or designee reviewed the draft report to ensure \par consistency with the Committee decision. The views and the decision of the \par rating committee are summarized in the above rationale and outlook. The \par weighting of all rating factors is described in the methodology used in this \par rating action (see 'Related Criteria And Research'). \par  \par RATINGS LIST \par  \par                                          Rating \par                                          To                  From \par Hungary \par  Sovereign Credit Rating \par   Foreign and Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2              BBB-/Positive/A-3   BBB-/Positive/A-3 \par  Transfer & Convertibility Assessment    BBB+                BBB+ \par  Senior Unsecured \par   Foreign and Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2              BBB-                BBB- \par  Short-Term Debt \par   Foreign and Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2              A-3                 A-3 \par  \par Hungary (National Bank of) \par  Sovereign Credit Rating \par   Foreign and Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2              BBB-/Positive/--    BBB-/Positive/-- \par  \par Ratings Subsequently Withdrawn \par  \par Hungary (National Bank of) \par  Sovereign Credit Rating \par   Foreign and Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc