\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 3- \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 , stocks recoup some losses after initial Brexit shock\b0\par\par\uc2 952 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2016\par 15:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Updates with markets recouping losses, mid-term outlook)\par     By Krisztina  Than\par     BUDAPEST, June 24 (Reuters) - Central European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par and stocks recouped some of their earlier steep losses by Friday\par afternoon as the initial shock from Britain's vote to leave the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  wore off, but markets remained volatile on uncertainty over\par the medium-term fallout.  \par     Direct trade linkages with Britain account for 3-6 percent\par of the foreign trade of central eastern European countries, with\par the euro zone having a much bigger role. But Britain is still\par Poland's second-biggest export market, with its exports to\par Britain in 2015 at around 12 billion euros.\par     Investors in Central Europe have been worried that the\par impact of Britain's exit could be bigger if it leads later to\par cuts in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  budget.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  development funds play a big role in boosting economic\par growth in poorer \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  states, so a reduction would be painful.\par There are also hundreds of thousands of east Europeans working\par in Britain.\par     However, some analysts said the region's strong growth\par potential still made emerging European assets attractive and\par further market losses would likely be contained, especially as\par there was further monetary policy easing globally.\par     "With central banks anchoring short-term rates at very low\par levels, central European bonds may rally once risk aversion\par subsides," \f28 \uc2 \f2 UniCredit\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts said in a note.\par     "Second, expected \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  support for periphery bonds should\par help stabilise bond prices in central Europe as well."     \par     Early in the morning the zloty tumbled to a 4-1/2\par year low of around 4.51 against the euro but later firmed and at\par 1206 GMT it traded 2 percent weaker from Thursday at 4.457. \par     The forint was one percent weaker. Hungarian five-\par and ten-year year government bond yields jumped 15-20 basis\par points, and there was a bearish steepening in the yield curve, a\par trader said.\par     Warsaw's WIG20 blue-chip index, which touched a\par 7-year low, later recouped some losses and was trading 7 percent\par lower at 1200 GMT. Prague stocks were down 6 percent. Budapest\par stocks were down 4 percent.\par     "The big question is what happens to those who work there\par (in the UK)," said Janos Samu, an analyst at Concorde Securities\par in Budapest. "What hits us badly now is all this uncertainty."\par     Other analysts were assessing the impact that Brexit could\par have on growth in eastern Europe.\par     "We see overall growth impact as biggest in (the) Czech\par (Republic) and then Hungary at around 0.5pp shaved off of growth\par under Brexit, Poland around 0.25 pp and other countries\par minimal," Peter Attard Montalto at Nomura said in a note.\par     Poland's deputy finance minister said the weakening of the\par zloty was not a risk to the Polish economy. He said he saw no\par reason to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. \par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it had all the necessary\par tools to continuously ensure financial stability. \par     The Czech crown, which is considered as a safe\par haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the region, eased only 0.1 percent.\par     Some analysts say Brexit could lead the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par to having to delay again its exit from a crown cap keeping the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weaker than 27 to the euro, a policy the bank has last\par said will last probably until the middle of 2017.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ceska Sporitelna\f28 \uc2 \f2 's chief economist, David Navratil, said\par slower growth in Germany and other euro zone trade partners\par because of Britain's looming \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  exit will hit the Czech economy\par as well, slowing inflation growth.\par     "It should affect GDP growth... and inflation. These two\par factors should postpone the exit (from the cap)," he said. "We\par expect they will postpone to the end of 2018."\par \par                CEE MARKETS  SNAPSHOT    AT  1403 CET             \par                             \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                            \par                             Latest     Previous    Daily   Change\par                             bid        close       change  in\par                                                            2016\par  Czech crown                  27.1050     27.0780  -0.10%  -0.39%\par  Hungary                     317.6200    314.6200  -0.94%  -0.94%\par  forint                                                    \par  Polish zloty                  4.4570      4.3675  -2.01%  -4.46%\par  Romanian leu                  4.5350      4.5245  -0.23%  -0.35%\par  Croatian                      7.5250      7.5252   +0.00   1.51%\par  kuna                                                   %  \par  Serbian                     123.6600    123.9600   +0.24  -1.77%\par  dinar                                                  %  \par  Note: daily   calculated   previous   close at    1800        