\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 1- \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  hover near highs, Serbia seen cutting rates\b0\par\par\uc2 873 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 2015\par 10:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Serbian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seen cutting rates by 25 or 50\par bps-poll\par     * Zloty off 3 1/2-year high, testing 4.0 vs euro\par     * Investors watch central banks as CEE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are strong\par \par     By Sandor Peto and Jakub Iglewski\par     BUDAPEST/WARSAW, April 9  (Reuters) - Central European\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  changed little on Thursday after a Wednesday's surge\par brought them to levels that may prompt central banks to curb\par their strength.\par     Serbia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to cut interest rates on\par Thursday as its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains strong, even though it sold\par dinars in recent weeks. A loan agreement with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International\par Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  opened the way for the bank to cut its key rate\par last month, by 50 basis points to 7.5 percent.\par     Most analysts in a Reuters poll forecast another cut for\par Thursday. The dinar gained 0.1 percent\par against the euro to 119.95 by 0739 GMT.\par     "While caution is surely well-advised amidst a turbulent\par global environment and remaining domestic uncertainties, pausing\par at this stage would unnecessarily postpone economic recovery,"\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a note.\par     Serbia has the highest interest rates in the region and its\par rate cuts aim at helping the weak economy. Elsewhere in the\par region, economic growth is buoying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's asset purchases also help, but\par concerns that the U.S. Federal Reserve may start raising rates\par in June caused wobbles in regional markets in recent weeks.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Poland's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said last month that it had finished\par its rate cuts. That has strengthened the zloty, which\par hit 3 1/2-year highs against the euro on Wednesday. It traded\par flat at 4.105 on Thursday, testing a key level at 4.0.\par     "We are getting close to the point where (Poland's) NBP will\par have to swallow the bitter pill and say something about the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," RBS analysts said in a note. "This may not be enough\par to stem the trend but should serve as a wake-up call."\par     A rebound of Russia's rouble has also helped the zloty, said\par Marcin Turkiewicz, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealing at mBank in Warsaw.\par     "We are linked to that market due to geopolitical\par proximity," he said, adding that the zloty may pierce 4.0 and\par even move to 3.97-3.96 this week.\par     The forint eased 0.1 percent against the euro to\par 297.50, after touching its strongest levels since late 2013 on\par Wednesday.\par     The leu was flat at 4.4, beyond which the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Romanian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb its strength.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is seen cutting rates further. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Romania's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may also ease monetary screws as the government has brought\par forward a deep cut in value-added tax on food to June.\par  \par     In the Czech Republic, the crown weakened to 27.36 to the\par euro -- keeping it within the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's cap on the\par crown's value of 27 to the euro. Appetite for testing the cap is\par fading, dealers and fund managers say. \par  *                                                            *\par  **************** CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 0939 CET ************ \par  ************************* \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  *************************\par                             Latest  Previous   Daily   Change  \par                             bid     close      change  in 2015 \par  Czech crown                 27.370    27.405   +0.13%  +1.03% \par  Hungary forint             297.500   297.205   -0.10%  +6.38% \par  Polish zloty                 4.015     4.015   -0.01%  +6.74% \par  Romanian leu                 4.400     4.398   -0.05%  +1.84% \par  Croatian kuna                7.593     7.605   +0.15%  +0.81% \par  Serbian dinar              119.950   120.050   +0.08%  +1.17% \par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1800 CET \par  **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                             Latest   Previous  Daily   Change  \par                                      close     change  in 2015 \par  Prague                      1049.11  1047.61   +0.14% +10.25% \par  Budapest                   20795.03 20804.91   -0.05% +25.89% \par  Warsaw                      2429.18  2424.82   +0.18%  +4.57% \par  Bucharest                   7119.91  7101.21   +0.26%  +3.19% \par  Ljubljana                    812.97   809.69   +0.41%  +4.29% \par  Zagreb                      1711.60  1709.83   +0.10%  -1.64% \par  Belgrade                       0.00   715.74   +0.00% -100.0% \par  Sofia                        512.24   512.76   -0.10%  -2.21% \par  ********