\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 1-Zloty extends rebound, minister's comments provide support\b0\par\par\uc2 802 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2014\par 11:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Zloty firms, finance minister says pace of fall worrying\par     * Forint flat, cbank may support it by year-end -dealers\par     * Dinar rises, \f28 \uc2 \f2 IMF\f28 \uc2 \f2  says Serbia budget in line with loan deal\par \par     By Sandor Peto\par     BUDAPEST, Dec 30 (Reuters) - The zloty firmed against the\par euro on Tuesday after Poland's finance minister Mateusz Szczurek\par said the pace of last week's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fall might give cause for\par concern.\par     Szczurek repeated late on Monday that he was not worried by\par the zloty's recent weakening to 4.3 to the euro, which would\par support the economy, but he saw cause for concern in the pace of\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's fluctuations. \par     Central Europe's most liquid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  extended its rebound\par on Tuesday for a second day, after losing almost 3 percent in\par December on expectations the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could cut interest\par rates again.\par     The zloty <EURPLN-=> firmed 0.2 percent to 4.305 against the\par euro by 0905 GMT after gaining on Monday as exporters bought the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par     It shed about 2.5 percent last week, and touched a\par two-and-1/2-year low at 4.3983, after a fall in Polish retail\par sales in November fuelled expectations that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par will resume interest rate cuts.\par     The Hungarian forint was flat at 315.35, off a\par three-year low of 317.40 hit last week.\par     Some market participants also expect Hungary to cut its\par record low 2.1 percent base rate further next year to help\par revive inflation and the slowing economy. \par     In the next few days, however, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par could still try to strengthen the forint via market\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  before the year-end to help the government reduce\par state debt from end-2013 levels, traders said.\par     A firmer forint reduces Hungary's debt as about 37 percent\par of the country's state debt, amounting to 24 trillion forints\par ($92.6 billion), is denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  never comments on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     "I guess the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could try to push the forint to\par 308-310 (by Wednesday's fixing) but I don't expect a bigger\par change, like past 305," one Budapest-based trader said.\par     The Serbian dinar firmed against the euro on\par Tuesday, by 0.1 percent to 121.09, after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International\par Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  said late on Monday that Serbia's 2015 budget was\par in line with the terms of a 1 billion euro ($1.22 billion) loan\par agreement with the Fund. \par     Passage of the budget has lifted confidence in the dinar,\par pushing it off record lows touched in mid-December, and the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which keeps the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in tight ranges,\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week to curb its rise.\par \par  *                                                            *\par  **************** CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1005 CET ************ \par  ************************* \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  *************************\par                             Latest  Previous   Daily   Change  \par                             bid     close      change  in 2014 \par  Czech crown                 27.708    27.720   +0.04%  -1.36% \par  Hungary forint             315.350   315.310   -0.01%  -5.83% \par  Polish zloty                 4.305     4.313   +0.18%  -3.61% \par  Romanian leu                 4.477     4.471   -0.14%  -0.37% \par  Croatian kuna                7.659     7.660   +0.01%  -0.54% \par  Serbian dinar              121.090   121.200   +0.09%  -5.44% \par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1800 CET \par  **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                             Latest   Previous  Daily   Change  \par                                      close     change  in 2014 \par  Prague                       944.76   946.27   -0.16%  -4.27% \par  Budapest                   16538.53 16519.16   +0.12% -09.79% \par  Warsaw                      2299.55  2302.59   -0.13%  -4.36% \par  Bucharest                   6956.17  7015.23   -0.84%  +7.38% \par  Ljubljana                    769.98   774.56   -0.59% +20.12% \par  Zagreb                      1736.37  1735.23   +0.07%  -2.47% \par  Belgrade                       0.00   654.53   +0.00% -100.0% \par  Sofia                        528.15   528.14   +0.00%  +7.48% \par  ***************************** BONDS **************************\par                         Yield    Yield    Spread     Daily     \par                         (bid)    change   vs Bund    change in \par  Czech Republic                                      s