\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-Dinar hits two-week high as Serbia vows spending cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 1,077 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2014\par 16:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Adds bond markets, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate meetings)\par     * Budget cut plan lifts Serbia's dinar, stocks\par     * CEE assets mostly up, bonds firm as Scots decide to stay\par in UK\par     * Hungarian, Czech central banks seen holding fire next week\par \par     By Sandor Peto and Ivana Sekularac\par     BUDAPEST/BELGRADE, Sept 19  (Reuters) - The dinar jumped to\par a two-week high against the euro on Friday after Serbia\par announced spending cuts that may open the way to credit talks\par with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2  (IMF).\par     Serbia and neighbouring Croatia are struggling with\par recession and high budget deficits. Measures announced by\par Serbian Prime Minister Aleksandar Vucic late on Thursday offer\par Belgrade the chance of an escape from rising debt. \par     Central European assets mostly gained after Scottish voters\par rejected independence, ending the threat of the United Kingdom's\par breaking up, which had created uncertainty throughout European\par markets.\par     The Belgrade stock exchange led a rise, with the index of\par its top 15 shares surging 1.6 percent, while a 3 percent dip of\par volatile \f28 \uc2 \f2 Magyar Telekom\f28 \uc2 \f2  stocks pulled down Budapest's\par main index by 0.8 percent.\par     The dinar, which had dropped to two-year lows to\par the euro in recent months as Serbia put off fiscal reforms,\par gained a quarter of a percent to 118.25.\par     "The announcement of saving measures lifted the dinar," one\par Belgrade-based dealer said. \par     The Serbian prime minister announced pension and public-\par sector pay cuts on Thursday, hoping to shrink a budget deficit\par of more than 8 percent of output and convince the IMF to agree a\par new loan deal. \par     Vucic "will hope that the structural reforms being pushed\par forward will deliver growth quickly enough to allow Serbia to\par grow out of its debt sustainability concerns," said Timothy Ash,\par analyst of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in a note.\par     Serbia's public debt is more than 70 percent of economic\par output. Record floods in the Balkans in May led to more\par spending, pushing Serbia into recession, and the region is again\par struggling with serious floods.\par     "Even though these are steps in the right direction, one\par should not ignore the fact that the measures and budget\par rebalance are delayed and thus reducing the chances of securing\par the IMF deal in this year," Hypo-Alpe-Adria bank said in a note.\par     "We see the EUR/RSD upwards potential staying in place given\par dinar negative factors stemming from banks' external\par de-leveraging and aggravated goods (foreign) trade trends," the\par bank said. "However, we don't see any abrupt EUR/RSD rise given\par the NBS (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) active liquidity management, both via \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and weekly REPO auctions."\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's forint rose 0.2 percent against the euro\par to 311.15, briefly touching eight-week highs at 310.30. It\par extended Thursday's 0.2 percent gain amid hopes that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard &\par Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  would raise the country's credit rating on Friday. \par     Hungary's debt is the highest in central Europe, at around\par 80 percent of gross domestic product. But its economy has been\par growing robustly, bucking the regional trend of economic\par slowdown.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  has a '\f28 \uc2 \f2 BB\f28 \uc2 \f2 ' rating on Hungary's long-term\par foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt. It improved the outlook to "stable" from\par "negative" on March 28. \par     "However, in our assessment it is more likely that the\par rating agency will not change our country's rating," \f28 \uc2 \f2 CIB Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par analysts said in a note.\par     \par     BONDS FIRM\par     Hungarian bond yields dropped as much as 10 basis points\par along the curve and Poland's 10-year yield fell 4\par basis points to 3.05 percent. \par     Traders said the bonds were helped by a relief in euro zone\par bond markets after the Scottish referendum.\par     The region's bonds may now resume their upward trend, which\par was interrupted in the recent weeks by worries that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal\par Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  was shifting to a more hawkish stance.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2 's meeting on Wednesday did not suggest a big change\par anytime soon, one Budapest-based fixed income trader said.\par     "Bond yields are now about 20 basis points from record lows\par and Poland is similar," the trader added. "The question now is\par what people will think if yields go 10-15 basis points lower."\par     The Hungarian and Czech central banks will hold rate-setting\par meetings next week. Neither is expected to change its record-low\par interest rates.    \par  *                                                            *\par  **************** CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1549 CET ************ \par  ************************* \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  *************************\par                             Latest  Previous   Daily   Change  \par                             bid     close      change  in 2014 \par  Czech crown                 27.559    27.532   -0.10%  -0.82% \par  Hungary forint             311.150   311.875   +0.23%  -4.56% 