\par\par \b \uc2 European Summary: Ukraine Ceasefire Lifts Sentiment -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2014\par 14:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1232 GMT Reports of a ceasefire between Ukraine and Russia triggered a sharp lift in sentiment on Wednesday. In \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro was back under pressure after a disappointing run of services PMI data, hitting a fresh one-week high vs the dollar, the ruble climbed more than 1% vs USD while the Swiss franc gave a little ground. Sterling continued to be weigh by Scottish referendum nerves, a strong 60.5 print for the services PMI giving the pound limited relief. Equities bourses spiked higher with the CAC40 and DAX30 holding gains of 1.2%, Moscow's Micex added 2.7%. (gary.stride@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 8:18 EDT - Treasury fall as tensions ease regarding Ukraine amid word of a cease-fire agreement with Russia, though it's unclear if it's permanent or not. Ten-year notes are down 9/32 to yield 2.45%, adding to Tuesday's sharp decline. Bond traders say much of August's rally had more to do with haven flows than economic fundamentals. While this month hasn't been a good start for Treasurys, US employment data later this week and the ECB's policy statement Thursday will set a more-concrete direction. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1136 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Foreign banks with subsidiaries in Hungary may consider a partial or total withdrawal from the country due to the losses they'll incur as a result of the refunds they have to pay to foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mortgage borrowers, says IRS Banking Risk Service. The may quit "even if this means selling assets at a discount," says IRS economist Antonio Timoner-Salva. The results of the upcoming European stress test and asset quality review of systemically important banks will also provide the basis for any potential bank deleveraging in emerging Europe, with Hungary, Ukraine, and Balkan countries among the most likely targets for divestment, he adds. (margit.feher@wsj.com @margitfeher) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:36 EDT - A limited poll of Brazilian voters shows Silva has taken the lead in 2 major states--Sao Paulo and Rio de Janeiro. According to a survey by the Ibope polling institute, she's running ahead of Rousseff 39-23% in Sao Paulo and 38-32% in Rio. Combined, the 2 states have nearly 1/3 of Brazil's voters. The results could further help asset prices as investors have been enthused about the prospect of a new president being elected next month. Rousseff has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in key economic sectors while the main opposition candidate is considered more market friendly. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:31 EDT - Investors seem to have their bull faces on today between strong gains in oil and equities and a continued pullback in sovereign debt. As profits are taken by crude shorts and bond bulls, it's easy to get too giddy about the state of things. But Europe's economy is still stuck in the mud, whether or not the Ukraine mess is hurting things that much more. Yet for today, it seems the sun rose brightly. It could be a whole different scene in just over 24 hours. As oil rebounds 1% after yesterday's drubbing, S&P 500 futures are up 8 and gold is slightly higher. The euro has moved back above $1.3150 and the 10-year Treasury yield has risen to 2.45%. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1127 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Portugal's new 2030-dated government bond fills a gap on the country's yield curve as it doesn't have any publicly issued government bonds maturing between 2024 and 2037. The issue size of the new bond is likely to be set at EUR3.5 billion. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1125 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may need to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the HUF market at the end of the year to comply with its constitutional obligations and EU demands to reduce public debt, \f28 \uc2 \f2 UniCredit\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Dan Bucsa says. About 40% of Hungary's debt is in a foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  thus a stronger HUF would lower its debt in forints as a percentage of GDP. Hungary's public debt was at a four-year high of 85.1% of GDP at end-June, which would need to be reduced to 79.4% of GDP by year-end. \f28 \uc2 \f2 UniCredit\f28 \uc2 \f2  is looking for EUR/HUF at close to or below 308 by end-2014. EUR/HUF is at 313.91. (margit.feher@wsj.com @margitfeher) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:19 EDT - Data the past 24 hours have done little to distract from the sharp picture of divergence between the US and the rest of the world, which has been driving the dollar higher. However, more broadly it has at least softened concerns about the overall global economy as the numbers show growth in pockets around the world. Europe's service-sector PMIs were on average slightly weaker than expected, with Germany continuing to be a worry--which contrasts with the booming report on US manufacturing yesterday. But with strong growth in Spain and the UK, it's not a monolithic picture of pain in Europe. And China's \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  services index showed a welcome rebound from a dismal readout in July. While this won't detract from the buy-the-dollar argument, it's nothing to panic about, either. (michael.casey@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:13 EDT - After sharp gains in recent years, housing prices in Brazil are showing signs of stabilization, although they continue to outpace inflation. Home prices have risen an average 4.8% since the start of 2014 while inflation has been 4% the past 8 months, according an index based on prices in Brazil's 16 largest cities. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1108 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Any potential \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  program to buy asset-backed securities to boost lending to small and medium-size companies may be too small unless it buys other types of ABS, according to credit analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 JPMorgan\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Just EUR10.3 billion of ABS linked to SME debt are outstanding, they say; half of which consists of pre-crisis Spanish exposures. That means the ECB will either need to buy non-corporate assets, such as residential mortgages, or encourage the structuring of SME-backed deals on a scale never seen before, the analysts say. (ben.edwards@wsj.com @debtnerd) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1105 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The August U.K. services PMI came in significantly better than expected, rising to 60.5 from 59.1 in July, shoring up the view taken by \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  that the U.K. macro outperformance remains intact. Meanwhile, eurozone 3Q data continues to disappoint, with its service sector PMI slipping to 53.1 from 53.5 in July. This divergence keeps the French bank happy with its EUR/GBP short recommendation, although it has tightened the stop on the trade to the 0.8020 entry point. EUR/GBP now at 0.7979. (gary.stride@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1104 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--A predicted slowdown in Europe will likely affect Hungary, with the expected result a strong deceleration in industrial production in the second half of 2014, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 UniCredit\f28 \uc2 \f2 , after the Hungarian statistics office confirms 2Q GDP growth of 0.8% Q/Q and 3.9% Y/Y. Even so, GDP is expected to grow by about 3.2% in 2014, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 UniCredit\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Dan Bucsa. Hungary's need to meet EU-imposed fiscal targets will weigh on growth next year, with GDP growth slowing to close to 2.1% Y/Y, he adds. "As the government continues to focus on acquiring private companies in the utility and financial sectors and supporting public employment schemes, the scope for investment spending will narrow," Mr. Bucsa says. (margit.feher@wsj.com @margitfeher) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 03, 2014 08:32 ET (12:32 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140903ea93001jh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
