\par\par \b \uc2 Canadian Dollar Rallies on Strong Domestic Data -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,280 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 2014\par 15:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 9:06 EDT - The Canadian dollar extends earlier gains after a duo of strong domestic reports, with June CPI and May wholesale sales rising more than expected. In theory, the CPI increase shouldn't be that supportive for the loonie since the Bank of Canada made it abundantly clear Wednesday it considers the recent uptick in inflation to be transitory, and today's numbers weren't that higher than expected. But with wholesale-sale growth nearly quadrupling expectations, the combination gave a nice jolt of positive energy to the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The US dollar is at C$1.0730 versus C$1.0761 late Thursday. (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 1255 GMT [Dow Jones]--\uc2 UniCredit\uc2  expects Italy to cancel some upcoming government bond auctions, says the bank's rates strategist Luca Cazzulani. He expects the Italy Treasury to cancel the mid-August auction of conventional government bonds, or BTPs, which is scheduled for Aug. 13, and possibly also the end-July auction of inflation-linked bonds, scheduled for July 28. August is traditionally the month with the lowest gross government bond supply during the year. Several countries either don't plan bond auctions at all or cancel some of them. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 8:49 EDT - The haven bond prices higher again, reflecting a search for safety ahead of the weekend, given geopolitical risks in Ukraine and Middle East. Continued slide in German bund yield adds to the lure of Treasurys as US offers much higher yields. "If we look solely at domestic economic fundamentals, then yields will probably rise slowly and gradually," says Gary Pollack at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's private wealth management unit. "However, the \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  market is truly an international market." He expects yields to stay low, and adds, "we could test 2.40%" -- this year's low set in late May. The 10-year note 2/32 higher, yielding 2.466%; 10-year German bund's yield fell to 1.14%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 1248 GMT [Dow Jones]--\b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies could backfire and slow down GDP growth, says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 . Cites public acquisitions of utility companies and the centralization of public services, which it says will crowd out private investment. Government's plan to aid households with \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans will weaken the banking sector significantly, thus "a lending-driven recovery of domestic demand becomes very unlikely" and "the government faces the risk of fiscal costs if it has to bail out some of the weaker banks," says \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  economist Dan Bucsa. Expects Hungary's GDP growth to slow down from here in the coming quarters after +3.5% on the year in 1Q, and sees it at 2.9% on the year in 2014 and 2.0% on the year in 2015. (margit.feher@wsj.com @margitfeher)\par \par \uc2 8:40 EDT - Accelerating Canadian inflation could make it hard for the Bank of Canada to continue asserting that price gains are temporary, as it most recently did Wednesday. Consumer prices rose more than expected in June, and 2Q's average CPI of 2.2% was above the BoC's boosted 2.1% forecast from 2 days ago. The average core rate of 1.6% matched the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest estimate. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com, @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 1237 GMT [Dow Jones]--Geopolitical risks in emerging markets are bringing EM \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  back to life. This strengthens the case to differentiate across markets, says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . "We stay bearish CLP, while MXN and COP have outperformed," the bank's analysts say. They also see scope for near-term weakness in BRL. Meanwhile, volatility in aggregate remains near historical lows, and developed market assets have not come under pressure because of geopolitical factors. (chiara.albanese@wsj.com @chiaraalbanese)\par \par \uc2 1229 GMT \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  reckons that the Hungarian authorities still favor a gradual depreciation of the forint since a more rapid one threatens the reduction of the public debt-to-GDP ratio. Hungary must reduce its debt ratio to keep its access to vital EU development funds. Despite the country's lower borrowing plans and cuts to public spending, EUR/HUF above 308 at the end of the year would keep the debt-to-GDP ratio above last year's level of 79.3% of GDP, notes \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  economist Dan Bucsa. Sees \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  indirect \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  into EUR/HUF rate likely toward end-2014, when the debt-to-GDP level is defined, should HUF fail to ensure debt reduction. EUR/HUF is at 309.80. (margit.feher@wsj.com @margitfeher)\par \par \uc2 1227 GMT [Dow Jones] Portugal's 5.65% February 2024-dated government bond should outperform the recently issued U.S. dollar denominated October 2024-dated Portuguese bond by 20-25 basis points on a cross-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjusted basis, in the coming months, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  rates strategist David Schnautz. He says this should also be supported by sizable coupon and redemption payments from Italy and Spain. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 8:26 EDT - For the first time in two years, consumer confidence in Chile has fallen into negative territory, polling firm Adimark says. Their index on consumer confidence fell to 49.2% in June, marking the sixth straight monthly decline. The pollster says the economic slowdown has finally reached consumers. Government officials expect the economy to expand by about 3.0% this year, down from a 4.1% expansion last year. Adimark says its poll shows concerns about employment and wages, while automobile and real estate markets are weakening. A debate over tax increases is also likely weighing on consumers, it added. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:26 EDT - Macroeconomic headwinds in certain Latin America and Caribbean countries prompted more negative sovereign-rating actions than positive ones in 1H, says Fitch. "Softer commodity prices, China's slowdown, slower domestic demand and country-specific factors are clouding the near-term prospects." Fitch is projecting Latin America's real GDP growth will slow to 1.9% in 2014 from last year's 2.6%, with only modest recovery expected in 2015. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:23 EDT - Canadian consumer price data for June due at 8:30am ET. Economists expect all-items inflation will be 2.3% on a yearly basis, same reading as May, and believe core inflation was 1.7% y/y, also unchanged from May. \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  says risks to CAD appear "asymmetrically negative." A strong print will likely be viewed as transitory -- as the Bank of Canada is viewing the recent rise inflation -- and not lift CAD much, firm says. "A disappointing print would reinforce the Bank's bias and should see CAD sell off," RBC adds. USD/CAD at 1.0744 vs 1.0761 late Thursday. (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 1219 GMT Ratings company \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says that \uc2 Liberty Global PLC\uc2 's purchase of a stake in \uc2 ITV plc\uc2  is credit negative for the U.K. broadcaster. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says \uc2 Liberty Global\uc2  is using debt to buy the stake, even though it's debt levels are already stretched, and it faces a challenging environment in some of its core cable TV markets such as the Netherlands. On Thursday, \uc2 Liberty Global\uc2  said it was buying a stake in \uc2 ITV\uc2  from \uc2 British Sky Broadcasting Group PLC\uc2  for GBP481 million ($824.18 million). \uc2 Liberty Global\uc2  did say, however, that it doesn't intend to make an offer to acquire \uc2 ITV\uc2 . Share is \uc2 ITV\uc2  are up 0.6% at 196p. (josie.cox@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 18, 2014 09:06 ET (13:06 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140718ea7i001pn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
