\par\par \b \uc2 What To Expect from Another Hungary Fidesz Government? -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Margit Feher  \par \uc2 1,047 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 2014\par 18:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 The incumbent Fidesz party is widely expected to win Hungary's general elections handily on Sunday, possibly matching its current two-thirds majority in parliament. Since it took over government in 2010, the party has tightened its grip on the country's legal system and economy, picked fights with the \uc2 European Union\uc2  with a new constitution that critics say limits basic rights and imposed a statist economic policy, much to the consternation of the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and foreign investors. Here is a look at what analysts say a second consecutive four-year term in power could bring:\par \par \uc2 Eurasia Group: Fidesz Win Would Be Investor-Negative\par \par \uc2 With a feeble center-left opposition, Fidesz looks likely to win the election and "that means more negative news for Hungary and for investors," said Eurasia Group analyst Mujtaba Rahman. Eurasia gives Fidesz a 50% chance of getting another supermajority.\par \par \uc2 "Another supermajority will serve to validate Fidesz's dismantling of checks and balances since 2010 and likely push the political system further toward centralization," Mr. Rahman added. Even without a Fidesz supermajority, Hungary's macroeconomic conditions will remain mixed and could cause "the business environment to deteriorate in the short, medium and long term."\par \par \uc2 SocGen: Simple Fidesz Majority Would Be Positive\par \par \uc2 The election surprise at this point may well be if Fidesz only manages to win a simple majority, which would be a marked positive for market confidence and for Hungarian assets, as it "means that Fidesz would be unable to ram through legislative changes with impunity," \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  economist Phoenix Kalen said.\par \par \uc2 "Investors remain uncomfortable with Orb\u225\'00\'E1n's increasingly autocratic rule, his philosophical turn away from Western Europe, his administration's ad-hoc style of policy-making, frequent changes to the country's constitution, election promises of further bail-out packages for households with foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mortgages, penalizing treatment of targeted private sectors, and the accompanying erosion of business confidence and private investments," Ms. Kalen added.\par \par \uc2 BBH: Fiscal Stringency May Prove Hard\par \par \uc2 "Markets dislike Orb\u225\'00\'E1n and his Fidesz party for its confrontational posture with the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and the financial system in the past, which included arbitrary taxes on various industries, hurting utility companies by cutting energy costs, undermining the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  independence, forcing the early retirement of judges who don't conform to the party's views," said \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  economist Win Thin.\par \par \uc2 All that said, it is undeniable that Hungary's economic fundamentals are on good footing, and improving in many aspects. Inflation is low, growth is picking up, unemployment is falling, and the current account is moving further into surplus.\par \par \uc2 "An argument can be made that the Fidesz choices are just standard populism and that their true costs will not surface for years to come --but when they do surface, history will be re-written," he added.\par \par \uc2 Nomura: Euro Adoption by End-2018 Unlikely\par \par \uc2 Under Fidesz, a streak of nationalism has become deep-rooted and euro-adoption at the end of its next parliamentary period--2018--looks unlikely, said Nomura economist Peter Attard Montalto. There will be low growth but higher inflation, with foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt reform probably the biggest area of uncertainty for markets.\par \par \uc2 The key will be if--in order to pay for personal income tax and small and medium-size companies' tax cuts--there are additional changes to the special taxes levied on larger companies, keeping policy uncertainty high for foreign direct investors, he added.\par \par \uc2 Expect an increase in the amount of government debt held by locals: Fidesz will want more bank assets domestically owned and more key utilities and infrastructure to end up in domestic hands.\par \par \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  : More of the Same\par \par \uc2 Fidesz's election program includes a consolidation of policies introduced in its current term, and the continuation of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking and energy sectors. "These policies will not be viewed positively by foreign investors and could worsen the investment climate, " said \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  economist Gautam Kalani.\par \par \uc2 A supermajority would also enable Fidesz to make further amendments to the constitution, including altering the electoral system, which would be difficult to reverse in the longer term, Mr. Kalani added. On the fiscal front, there may be some slippage but the budget deficit is likely to remain below the 3% of GDP threshold, he said.\par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  : A Less Eventful Term\par \par \uc2 "We seriously doubt that the next four years will be as eventful as the last four," said \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  economist Pasquale Diana. It seems likely to us that Fidesz will continue to put downward pressure on regulated energy prices, and seek a larger role for the state to play in the energy sector, which could hamper investment. We see no case for Fidesz to indicate a preference for a weaker Hungarian forint," Mr. Diana said, citing that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength is no obstacle to exports, a weak forint policy would be hard to control and that the government is still heavily indebted in forints.\par \par \uc2 4Cast: Risk of Weaker Forint\par \par \uc2 Financial markets will regard a two-third majority election victory as slightly more negative than a simple majority, said 4Cast economist G\u225\'00\'E1bor Ambrus. "Risks remain that the government--in tacit cooperation with the \uc2 National Bank of Hungary\uc2 --would continue to want to keep the forint on the back foot in order to kick start growth and we think that any solution for a drastic reduction in the remaining household foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt level would point to this direction."\par \par \uc2 Citi: Would Fidesz Team With Far Right?\par \par \uc2 A potential source of surprise could come from Fidesz failing to win a simple majority in parliament, which may force it to seek coalition with the far-right Jobbik party--a clearly unwelcome event for markets, said \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  economist Eszter G\u225\'00\'E1rgy\u225\'00\'E1n.\par \par \uc2 Over the next four years, Hungary's economic growth may continue to benefit from firming European growth, slowing deleveraging, and the country's accelerated absorption of \uc2 European Union\uc2  support funds. But \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  government policy could keep \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's growth prospects weak relative to its regional peers, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Margit Feh\u233\'00\'E9r at margit.feher@wsj.com \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at Wall Street Journal's Emerging Europe blog,  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/ }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April 04, 2014 12:33 ET (16:33 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140404ea44002mo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
