\par\par \b \uc2 Central Banks Take Advantage of Calm in Markets\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brian Blackstone and Emre Peker   \par \uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Februar 2014\par 17:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held interest rates steady and Hungary cut rates for a 19th straight month while leaving the door open to do more, as central bankers took advantage of the recent calm in financial markets to shore up their economies.\par \par \uc2 Still, the fragilities that sparked a rout in emerging markets last month such as high financing needs and weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  haven't been resolved, analysts warned, and could reignite turmoil in many developing economies.\par \par \uc2 "Some of the vulnerabilities that caused the selloff [in emerging markets last month] are still around," said William Jackson, emerging markets economist at consultancy Capital Economics. "It's premature to say that they're out of the woods."\par \par \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday kept its benchmark one-week rate at 10%, as widely expected. Policy makers also left the overnight borrowing rate at 8% and the overnight lending rate at 12%.\par \par \uc2 "Inflation indicators are forecast to remain above the 5% target for a little while longer...The tight monetary stance will continue until a visible improvement is achieved in the inflation outlook," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The decision came three weeks after an emergency meeting resulted in dramatic rate increases as policy makers scrambled to halt an emerging markets selloff that pushed its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the lira, to record lows. The increases also helped restore the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's credibility amid concerns that it had bowed to political pressure to keeps rates too low.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's lira was flat after the anticipated decision. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has rallied by almost 5% against the dollar since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's emergency rate rise, the biggest gain among emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed its credibility in a serious manner with its radical move on Jan. 28," said Nilufer Sezgin, chief economist at Erste Securities in Istanbul.\par \par \uc2 Still, "what roiled markets and caused the volatility was Turkey's large current-account deficit, its short-term financing and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves that didn't meet funding requirements. These factors are still in play," Ms. Sezgin said. The current account gap, which hit $65 billion in December, the highest since April 2012, should come down due to slower domestic demand and the weaker lira, she said.\par \par \uc2 The storm that rocked emerging market assets from Brazil to South Africa to Turkey this year has settled in recent weeks. Investors have become more comfortable with the U.S. Federal Reserve's plan to gradually withdraw its stimulus, while some emerging market central banks have fought back with rate increases and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to cut interest rates by 0.15 percentage points to a record-low 2.7% highlights the differences among developing economies, even as many fell victim to the same stampede in January.\par \par \uc2 Unlike Turkey and South Africa, which run high trade and investment deficits, Hungary runs a current account surplus. Consumer prices were flat last month on an annual basis, far below the bank's 3% target.\par \par \uc2 "Hungary's position is fundamentally strong compared to other emerging market economies," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday, opening the door to additional rate cuts. "Delivering price stability in the medium term points in the direction of monetary easing."\par \par \uc2 Still, Hungary still must finance a significant amount of external debt, making it sensitive to shifts in investor sentiment toward emerging markets. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the forint, fell after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision, with the euro up 0.8% at 310.87 forints.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "is playing with fire," said Nomura economist Peter Attard Montalto. If emerging markets remain in an episodic wobble over the coming months or even year, each rate cut will mean the next wobble weakens the Hungarian economy further and leaves it at a greater risk of capital outflows, he said.\par \par \uc2 "They seem to have gotten away with cutting interest rates," said Mr. Jackson of Capital Economics. "The test would come if the forint comes under more pressure."\par \par \uc2 Margit Feher in Budapest and Tommy Stubbington in London contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brian Blackstone at brian.blackstone@wsj.com and Emre Peker at emre.peker@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 18, 2014 11:43 ET (16:43 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140218ea2i002fz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
