\par\par \b \uc2 EU Court Says  \highlight22\uc2 Hungary \highlight\uc2  Can  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Loans -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Margit Feher  \par \uc2 795 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2014\par 15:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 A senior attorney at the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's top court issued a formal legal opinion Wednesday which could clear the way for Hungarian courts to rule on whether exchange rate conditions of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans were unfair.\par \par \uc2 The opinion came as \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Constitutional Court discusses how deep the government can \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  retroactively in the existing loans.\par \par \uc2 At the end of July 2013, there were around half a million foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mortgage contracts in Hungary worth 3.5 trillion forints ($15.38 billion), according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Fidesz party government, which is seeking a second consecutive term in power in elections in eight weeks' time, has said it wants to implement a relief system to ease the burden on borrowers who took out the loans before 2010 and now face high payments after a dramatic slide of the forint.\par \par \uc2 Foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans were vastly popular in Hungary prior to the financial crisis because they were much cheaper to service than forint loans. But the matter became a political issue following the sharp appreciation of the Swiss franc during the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European Court of Justice\uc2  will issue a ruling on the case at a later date, but it often follows the opinions of its advocates general. If the opinion forms the basis of the ruling, Hungarian courts will be able to change foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mortgage loans if they find exchange-rate stipulations in the loan contracts unfair.\par \par \uc2 Court decisions annulling the contracts may lead to potentially detrimental consequences for the borrowers as such decisions would force them to pay back the loan in one sum immediately, said Nils Wahl, Advocate General of the \uc2 European Court of Justice\uc2 . Hungarian courts easing the loan conditions with modifying the exchange rates would put some of the consequences of the forint's slide on mostly foreign-owned banks in Hungary.\par \par \uc2 In May 2008, the Kasler family took out a 14.4 million-forint ($63,365) mortgage with OTP, Hungary's biggest bank, with the mortgage calculated in Swiss francs. The contract stated that the amount in Swiss francs was to be calculated on the basis of the buy rate of the bank on the day the funds were made available.\par \par \uc2 However, reimbursement was to be calculated on the basis of the sell rate of the bank the day before the payment was due. The difference between these two rates was in the bank's favor.\par \par \uc2 The borrowers challenged this system before Hungarian courts, claiming that it was an abusive clause, an example of an illegal clause detrimental to consumers. The Hungarian Supreme Court, which is now hearing the case, turned to the \uc2 European Court of Justice\uc2 , which interprets EU law to make sure it is applied coherently throughout the bloc.\par \par \uc2 It asked the Luxembourg-based court to rule whether the clause in question was indeed abusive and if so, whether the Hungarian judge can modify it. A complete elimination of the clause would mean that the consumer would have to reimburse the mortgage immediately, in its entirety, which would be beyond the financial capabilities of most borrowers.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Wahl said Wednesday Hungarian courts could replace the clause, if they find it inadmissible in contracts, with other provisions in the Hungarian law.\par \par \uc2 "Where the deletion of an unfair term would make performance of the contract impossible, as in the present case, the directive does not preclude the national court from replacing the term at issue with a supplementary provision of national law, where such a replacement is possible under national law," Mr. Wahl said.\par \par \uc2 He also said foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mortgage borrowers must understand not only the content of the loan contracts but also the related obligations and rights. In the Kasler case, the contract conditions were set out in plain and simple language, it seems, but there may be doubts as to whether the borrower was "in the position to understand" that he would be subject to additional expense stemming from the difference between the selling and the buying price of the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mr. Wahl added.\par \par \uc2 In Mr. Wahl's view, Hungary's Supreme Court is to answer that question "in the light of the objective information available when the contract was concluded."\par \par \uc2 The Court of Justice ruling, which may follow several months from now, may differ from the opinion.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect that the Hungarian Supreme Court's decision--the final ruling in the case--will only come at end-May or in June.\par \par \uc2 Write to Margit Feher at margit.feher@wsj.com \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at Wall Street Journal's Emerging Europe blog,  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/ }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 12, 2014 09:41 ET (14:41 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140212ea2c002hw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwg