\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-Court opinion hits OTP shares, forint firmer\b0\par\par\uc2 912 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2014\par 17:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Adds Polish C/A, leu retreat, zloty-forint cross)\par     * EU court view of Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 fx\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loans hits OTP Bank shares\par     * Forint resilient, investors cut back selling positions\par     * Data show current accounts in region improved\par \par     By Sandor Peto\par     BUDAPEST, Feb 12 (Reuters) - Shares in Hungary's biggest\par bank fell on Wednesday after a European court opinion that could\par clear the way for Hungarian courts to challenge some aspects of\par contracts on foreign loans to households.\par     OTP Bank fell over 2 percent after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European\par Court of Justice\f28 \uc2 \f2  issued a non-binding legal opinion that under\par European law, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's national courts can \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  where\par contract terms are deemed unfair.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's government wants to implement a relief scheme for\par holders of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  mortgages which have become more\par costly to service as the forint has weakened, but is waiting for\par legal issues to be ironed out before adopting legislation.\par     The plan is expected to prove costly for the country's\par mostly foreign-owned banks, even though Hungary's supreme court\par has already ruled that lenders were not to blame when borrowers\par lost out because the exchange rate changed. The heavily-taxed\par banks' reluctance to lend is a drag on economic recovery.\par     But the ruling had only a limited impact on the forint,\par pressure on which has eased after last month's sell-off in\par emerging \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which forced Turkey and South Africa into\par emergency interest rate hikes. \par     The Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was bid at 308.21 versus\par the euro at 1521 GMT, up 0.7 percent from Tuesday but off the\par day's highs, with some investors reported to have closed short\par positions after the sell-off.\par     "I think the forint will consolidate unless problems emerge\par elsewhere (in other emerging markets) in the world," one\par Budapest-based \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealer said.    \par     The forint is seen as vulnerable as investors pull back from\par emerging markets. Although Hungary runs a current account\par surplus, making it less vulnerable than countries with big\par deficits such as South Africa and Turkey, it has high debt\par relative to Central European peers. \par     The Czech Republic and Poland reported lower than expected\par current account deficits for December on Wednesday, while\par Romania's deficit shrank about 75 percent to 1.5 billion euros.\par     The data helped Poland's zloty firm 0.4 percent\par against the euro. The leu shed 0.3 percent due to some\par bickering among ruling parties after last week's Romanian\par government reshuffle, but dealers did not expect further falls. \par  \par     \par     INFLATION FALLING\par     Forint investors are watching inflation data due on Friday\par for signals that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hungary's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could cut interest rates\par further from record lows of 2.85 percent despite the market\par tensions.\par     The forint hovered near record lows against Poland's zloty\par  around 74.10, and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Societe Generale\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Benoit Anne\par said in a note a forint rebound was unlikely given the policy\par stance.\par     Friday's data are expected to show inflation falling to new\par lows and possibly into negative territory.  \par     On Wednesday, Romania reported annual inflation at a record\par low 1.1 percent in January, down from 1.6 percent in December,\par while Czech inflation fell to its lowest since October 2009, at\par just 0.2 percent.  \par     The Czech inflation figures weakened the crown by\par 0.1 percent to 27.501 against the euro and underpinned the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy of using crown weakness to fight the\par threat of deflation. It knocked down the crown by over 7 percent\par with market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in November.\par     "Low inflation should keep the crown weaker and bond yields\par at rather low levels," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ceska Sporitelna\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Lubos\par Mokras in a note.\par                       CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1621 CET\par  ************************** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                              bid     close      change  in 2014\par  Czech crown                 27.502    27.474   -0.10%  -0.62%\par  Hungarian forint           308.210   310.440  +0.72%  -3.65%\par  Polish zloty                 4.156     4.173   +0.41%  -0.15%\par  Romanian leu                 4.484     4.473   -0.26%   -0.54%\par  Croatian kuna                7.651     7.650    -0.02%  -0.43%\par  Serbian dinar              115.670   115.800   +0.11%   -1.01%\par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1700 GMT\par  **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                                      close      change  in 2014\par  Prague                      1016.91    1002.07  +1.48% +3