\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-ANALYSIS-In emerging Europe, danger seen in policy rather than capital needs\b0\par\par\uc2 876 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2014\par 08:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2014 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Repeats, without changes, story first published on Tuesday)\par     * Hungary, Romania seen vulnerable in emerging Europe\par     * Politics, influence over central banks worry investors\par     * Hungary's debt, percentage of foreign ownership a worry\par     * Poland back in favour after pension policy storm\par \par     By Marc Jones\par     LONDON, Feb 11 (Reuters) - Central and eastern Europe may\par have weathered the latest emerging markets squalls better than\par most but Hungary and Romania show the region is not immune and\par capital flight is not the only worry on investors' minds.\par     While most central European markets have fared better over\par the past year than the 'fragile five' countries of Indonesia,\par South Africa, Brazil, India, and Turkey with their urgent need\par to finance current account gaps, many have still been dragged\par through the wringer over the past couple of weeks.\par     Hungary has made a particularly convincing case for turning\par the 'fragile five' into a 'shaky six'. \par     Despite Budapest's insistence that its current account\par surplus makes it far less reliant on the goodwill of foreign\par stock and bond investors, its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   and share\par market have sunk further than those of most in the\par quintet singled out for their acute dependence on investment\par flows.\par     The lesson seems to be that messy politics and weak central\par banks can be just as damaging for the image as deficits.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Aberdeen Asset Management\f28 \uc2 \f2  fund manager Viktor Szabo said\par Hungarian debt ratios near 80 percent of annual economic output,\par wrangles with foreign banks and a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  eyeing more\par interest rates cuts meant he was steering clear.     \par     "Hungary has no problem on the current account side ...\par (but) it has high debt levels, with a significant part of it\par denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," Szabo said.\par     "It is becoming more expensive to refinance the debt, and\par the risk is that another wave of global risk aversion could lead\par to failed bond auctions."\par     Hungary and Poland both sailed through bond sales on\par Thursday. But the forint remains near a 2-1/2 year low versus\par the euro and Hungary's borrowing costs \par have hardly budged from a six-month high, as has the premium\par investors demand to buy bonds there rather than from ultra-safe\par Germany or Poland.\par   \par                \par     POLAND 'THE BENCHMARK'\par     Romania and Serbia are showing some of the same strains as\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , although \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has helped limit the\par impact on the leu  and dinar \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par .\par     Daniel Hewitt, a senior emerging market economist at\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said the limited use of the dollar in the region\par should help but it would be unable to escape another big\par sell-off.\par     Individual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy stances are increasingly\par important for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  defence after abrupt defensive rate hikes\par in India, Turkey and South Africa.\par     Hungary's monetary policymakers, all appointed by the\par current government, have cut rates from a 7 percent 2012 peak to\par just to 2.85 percent last month.\par     They discuss interest rates again on Feb. 18 and with\par inflation extremely low - helped by energy price cuts - the bank\par has maintained there is more room to cut rates. But noises from\par politicians last week suggest the mood may be shifting.\par     "In my world, the difference between Hungary and Poland has\par been quite striking in this recent sell-off," said Hewitt.\par     "The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seems to have pushed monetary\par loosening too far ... and they have an overhang of liquidity due\par to their Funding for Growth scheme, whereas in Poland they have\par been pretty restrained."\par     Both Hewitt and Szabo tip Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and equities as best\par equipped to weather any further unrest.\par     Poland, the region's biggest emerging economy, has shaken\par off investor disapproval over a pensions nationalisation last\par year and its economy is seen growing almost 3 percent this year\par even though interest rates are higher than most. Its zloty\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has recouped all of its losses from the latest\par upset and its main stock index has rebounded.    \par     Its links to recovering core European economies like Germany\par give the zloty the natural advantage of a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  likely to\par be eyeing rate hikes later in the year.\par     "Poland is the real benchmark for me," added Murat Toprak,\par emerging market strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London. "It has good\par fundamentals ... if it starts selling off, then we also have to\par worry about a lot of different countries."\par         \par     UNION BENEFITS\par     As \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  members, Poland, Hungary and the Czech\par Republic are bound by the bloc's rules on fiscal house-keeping.\par     For the past several years that has constrained their\par governments from running big deficits to boost growth. \par     But it has also restrained current account deficits, sparing\par their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  the worst of the capital outflow risks posed by\par the U.S. retreat from easy money.\par     Then there are the EU's 'cohesion funds' to help\par ex-communist countries rebuild infrastructure. That's investment\par for which they don't have to compete with emerging economies\par elsewhere.\par     "The most important thing for these countries is (economic)\par growth. The data we have had recently has been good ...so they\par are positioned to outperform," said Hewitt at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     "If things do stabilise, even Hungary will strengthen."\par \par  (Additional reporting by Marcin Goettig in Warsaw and Sujata\par Rao in London; Editing by Ruth Pitchford)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-2-15T07:05:00.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140212ea2c00cmx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
