\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Hungary \highlight\uc2 : Verbal  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Can't Be Far Off -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,536 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2014\par 17:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1620 GMT [Dow Jones] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of Hungary\f28 \uc2 \f2  has come under pressure, given its ongoing easing bias in the face of pronounced underperformance of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  says. EUR/HUF rose to a two-year high at 313 earlier today; despite retreating to 311.65 in late afternoon profit-taking, the trend seems inevitable. While the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's tolerance for a weaker forint has clearly increased, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  says the current dynamics suggest that at least verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cannot be too far away. As always though, it won't be just when they say, it'll be what they say. (veronika.gulyas@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 10:56 EST - Capital Economics looks back at past Fed tightening cycles, which it notes often fueled "bouts of turmoil in emerging markets as global supply of cheap dollar funding began to dry up." Those periods, however, didn't have more than a temporary impact on US financial markets and its economy, which the firm notes is what the Fed cares about. CapEcon says the result this time should be no different. Of course today's Fed tapering is different in that it's unwinding years of extraordinary stimulus. But the firm still believes emerging markets as a whole will deliver moderate growth this year, and that if anything, the downturn in \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  yields will be supportive for domestic growth. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 10:47 EST - The \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  says volatility in global markets and its potential impact on the Mexican peso could have implications for inflation, which it expects to remain above its 4% target ceiling in coming months. Price increases resulting from new consumer taxes, meanwhile, could push medium-term expectations higher, so inflation risks have deteriorated, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says in leaving its overnight interest-rate target at 3.5%. US dollar last trading up 0.2% on the peso, at MXN13.395. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:38 EST - Looks like governments abroad in need of funds to support struggling \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have had to sell Treasurys. NY Fed's weekly flow data show Treasurys that foreign officials leave with the Fed -- referred to as custody holdings -- fell by nearly $21B in the week ended Jan. 29. That's the biggest weekly unloading since mid June. CRT says it doesn't read this as a bet against Treasurys since a number of emerging-market governments have needed to step up \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by buying their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid last week's sharp selloff. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 09:46 EST - Canada's solid month-over-month GDP gain in November should help quash speculation the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  might be eying a rate cut to juice economic growth, says \uc2 PNC Financial\uc2 . The 0.2% advance in November, due to gains in energy, mining and retail activity suggest Canada's economy "is growing nicely," said Bill Adams, economist at the bank. "The \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  has made its next monetary choice explicitly data-dependent -- and this data points to their next move being a hike, not a cut," he added, noting the weaker Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will eventually push the country's tepid inflation rate higher. (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 1436 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungary's official employment data showed Wednesday that more than four million people were working in the last quarter of 2103, a twelve-year peak. So far so good, but ING economist Andras Balatoni says the figures have undergone some 'plastic surgery,' being flattered by the 170,000 Hungarians on employment programs and others working abroad. He says that if those two categories are stripped out, the development of Hungary's labor market looks less impressive and is still far from normalization. (margit.feher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 9:28 EST - For the many who doubted Treasurys coming into 2014, the market has defied expectations, handing 1.25% in total January returns through Thursday. This was supposed to be the year of rising interest rates, yet the Fed's tapering seems to be catching up with global assets--and the great rotation seems to be working in favor of US bonds. "The interest in Treasurys against other assets that have richened so much over the last year-to-several years is a sentiment we've come across so consistently in the last several weeks," notes CRT's David Ader. Ten-year notes yield 2.65%, and Ader says for now interest should wane if the yield sinks to the 2.50s. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1427 GMT xx-DJ Amy Yuan Zhuang at Nordea points to Sunday's upcoming extraordinary election in Thailand - being conducted against the express recommendation of the election committee - potentially ending in tear gas and bloodshed. Protests have already been widespread since the New Year, and Bangkok has been a state of emergency zone since 21 January. "Police and pro-government groups will strike back to support the administration, but even in the absence of a collision, the political crisis is unlikely to be solved following the election. Downside risk to THB remains large", Ms Zhuang says. YTD the THB has only lost 1% vs. the dollar, but since last May all of the USD 6 billion equity inflows have disappeared - much more than any other Asian country. She goes on to say that ".. Although Thailand's economic fundamentals are fairly sound, the present bearish sentiment towards EMs as a whole - and the political crisis - will drive THB lower and upside potential is not good for 2014." USD/THB is 33.02 today: last April it was around 28.80, and the 2009 peaks around 36 must now be coming into focus. (christopher.tomkinson@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 09:14 EST - The dollar is stronger across most emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, leading some analysts to suggest that this may be a sign of month-end portfolio rebalancing. "Large investors are revisiting their exposures to risky assets and particularly emerging markets," said Sebastian Galy of \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . He suggests that lower growth forecasts for emerging economies, illiquidity and the continued withdrawal of capital out of these countries suggest that more investors are focused on bulking up on the dollar and developed-market assets. So far this month, EM equities are down 6.61% and EM \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  off 1.74%, based on MSCI indexes. (prabha.natarajan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1405 GMT [Dow Jones] The U.K.'s rather miserable productivity levels compared to its peers have been a puzzle for economists since the financial crisis. It was before, actually, but since then it has intensified as the local labor market held up well in all other respects. Capital Economics thinks that the 'dismal' productivity showing is likely to have continued in the old year's final quarter - but could now be set to improve, on rising corporate confidence and an uptick in demand. Let's hope so. Here on the trend-buckingly productive London Market Talk desk, we'll believe it when we see it. david.cottle@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 9:02 EST - The headline figure for November GDP in Canada came in line with expectations of a 0.2% monthly gain, but subindices push the loonie to hit fresh lows. While energy industries showed some strength, manufacturing and the agriculture and forestry sector declined, pushing USD/CAD above the 1.12 level for the first time since July 2009. That's opened the order books back up for USD buyers who believe there's more room for the pair to grind higher, said Jameson Bank's Tim Harling. A lot of people whoe were "willing to sell at 1.12 have revised their sell levels because they believe USD/CAD will go higher," he said. The next key technical figure for USD/CAD remains 1.1232, he added. The pair's at C$1.1220, up 0.57% for the day, according to CQG. (david.george-cosh@wsj.com @itsdgc)\par \par \uc2 1348 GMT [Dow Jones] February, a month with no redeeming euro zone government bonds, will see massive issuance in ultra-long debt--the segment with 15-year and longer maturity. Among the already known ultra-long issuance planned for February, Austria will tap its January 2062-dated government bond on Feb. 4, while France will reopen the October 2032-dated bond on Feb. 6. On Feb. 18, the Netherlands' will launch the new, January 2047-dated bond, known as DSL, at a direct auction---a system that (as the name implies) directly involves end-investors, with the state acting as single bookrunner. The Dutch plan to raise at least EUR 3 billion from the sale. On Feb. 26, Germany will launch the new August 2046-dated Bund at auction, with EUR3 billion. Ultra-long bonds are typically bought by pension funds and insurance firms, who hold them long-term, often until maturity. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 31, 2014 11:20 ET (16:20 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140131ea1v002tc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
