\par\par \b \uc2 EUR/HUF May Rise Until 320, Trader Says -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,263 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2014\par 11:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1015 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro-forint rate may rise further - even to 320 - until the \f28 \uc2 \f2 National Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  verbally \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , a local trader says. EUR/HUF is at 311.19 after testing a two-year high 312 earlier Thursday. The trader notes that there are many stop-loss positions around this level and trading currently points further upward. The 320 level is EUR/HUF's all-time high, hit in late 2011. (veronika.gulyas@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1013 GMT Poland's fourth quarter expansion accelerates to above 2.8% and could even surpass 3%, Millennium Bank writes, after full-year growth surprised with 1.6% expansion, against market expectations of 1.3%. Millennium sees ongoing expansion in the coming quarters partly thanks to the improving economic situation in the euro zone, rising investments and growing private consumption.(patryk.wasilewski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1011 GMT [Dow Jones] A mixed bag of UK data sees sterling extend the day's fall to fresh day lows of $1.6453 and EUR0.8264. Mortgage approvals were at their highest level for 6-years, but M4 money supply growth slowed sharply. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 0955 GMT [Dow Jones] Consumer confidence is expected to remain unchanged at a two-year high of -11.7. Business confidence should rebound to 0.36 from December's 0.27 print: economic sentiment should improve to 101.0 vs 100.0, while industrial confidence could edge higher to -2.5 vs -3.4. EUR/USD now at 1.3605, inline data may give it a small uptick, but while below 1.3660 the mood looks heavy. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 0939 GMT [Dow Jones] Italy's new five-year government bond, the 2.50% May 2019 BTP which will be launched at auction Thursday, "doesn't offer particular value in the five-year sector," says \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . Nonetheless, it has no concerns about the demand. "But a renewed appetite for peripherals may compensate for that in terms of demand," it says. Italy will offer up to a combined EUR 8.5 billion in the new 2019 BTP, as well as in the already existing March 2024 BTP and the November 2018 CCTeu, or floating rate note. Auction results are due at 1000 GMT. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0929 GMT [Dow Jones]US Treasurys miss out on the core bond rally as traders brace themselves for an unusual double auction this afternoon of USD 35bn 5-years and USD 29bn 7-years. European core bond markets continue Wednesday's risk-on rally with cash Bunds now trading around 1.605%, a level last seen in July 2013. Ahead, US 4Q 2013 GDP (1330 GMT) is expected to come in at a rather solid 3.4% gain after the 4.1% outcome in Q3-13, according to Annalisa Piazza at Newedge. "Personal consumption expenditure is expected to be the key supportive factor, as flagged by retail and vehicle sales data over the quarter." The March \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  contract is 2/32 lower at 125-180 and the 10-year cash yields 2.678%. (nick.cawley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0928 GMT [Dow Jones] Sterling prints fresh day lows vs USD, EUR ahead of December consumer lending data, which is expected to be upbeat. Consumer credit should expand to GBP 0.7bln from GBP 0.6bln in November, consumer lending rise to GBP 1.9bln from GBP 1.5bln, mortgage approvals increase to 73K from 71K and mortgage lending of GBP 1.2bln from GBP 0.9bln last. GBP/USD now at 1.6486, support is close by at 1.6480. EUR/GBP at 0.8252, resistance is 0.8260. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 0907 GMT [Dow Jones] Over the last few days four central banks have tightened policy (India, Turkey, S Africa, USA) and New Zealand signals its intention to join the pack, most likely in March. The reasons for tightening differ notes \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 : in EM countries it's concerns about financial imbalances and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness, while strong growth prospects have pushed the Fed to continue tapering and the RBNZ to signal a rate hike soon. The end result is the same: global liquidity is set to tighten and risky assets that benefited from abundant liquidity will suffer, as will commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as AUD, CAD and NZD. USD/CAD traded above 1.12 for the first time since July 2009, NZD/USD a near 1-month low of 0.8144 and AUD/USD at 0.8711. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 0900 GMT [Dow Jones] News that China's \uc2 Lenovo Group\uc2  (0992.HK) aims to acquire both Motorola's cellphone handset business and \uc2 IBM\uc2 's (\uc2 IBM\uc2 ) low-end server business was negative for several of Apple's (AAPL) iPhone parts suppliers in Japan Thursday. Shares of Ibiden (4062.TO) lost 3.2% to Y1,921, while Taiyo Yuden (6976.TO) fell 3.9% to Y1,271 and Rohm slipped 4.4% (6963.TO) to Y5,160--all underperforming the Nikkei's 2.5% fall. "It's entirely possible that Lenovo's move could be big and alter the landscape of the cell phone industry," says Advanced Research Japan analyst David Rubenstein. "Lenovo already has brand appeal in PCs and may be able to leverage that into the higher-end cell phone market. On the other hand, Samsung (005930.SE) is right on Apple's heels, while several Chinese players are already active in the lower-end phone space, so much will depend on where and how Lenovo aims its effort, he adds. For now, the market may be more cautious on Apple-related suppliers, as competition will likely only grow more intense going forward, he says. (bradford.frischkorn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0854 GMT [Dow Jones] The rise in the January Swiss KOF barometer suggests the economy is on a solid recovery path, says \uc2 Swissquote\uc2 . "A sturdier relationship between the KOF index and GDP looks to have redeveloped, indicating the slight pullback in GDP growth should be reversed," according to analyst Peter Rosenstreich. "Given the encouraging signs from the growth side, we think the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is underestimating inflation's trajectory and will have to adjust monetary policy sooner than anticipated," he points out. "From a relative value standpoint, we expect investors will favor Switzerland over Europe, and EUR/CHF could head back down to the 1.2180 level," he argues. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0847 GMT [Dow Jones] German jobless claims are expected to fall by 5K for January, which would leave the adjusted unemployment rate unchanged at 6.9%. EUR/USD now trades at 1.3616: the data is not usually a big \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mover, but just in case, levels to watch out for are the 1.3550 support line and resistance at 1.3655. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 0845 GMT [Dow Jones] Wednesday saw fresh support develop in many of the core bond markets, according to \uc2 UBS\uc2  technical strategist Richard Adcock, with any setbacks now offering new buying opportunities. In Europe \uc2 UBS\uc2  recommend going long March Bunds at 142.80 - currently trading at 143.09 - and going long gilts on a close above 110.00 - currently trading at 109.92. In the Treasury market, \uc2 UBS\uc2  suggest working bids at 125.09 to get long. March Treasurys trade at 125-145. (nick.cawley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 30, 2014 05:15 ET (10:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140130ea1u0018z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
