\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-Forint falls on CPI plunge, yield curves steepen\b0\par\par\uc2 881 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2013\par 17:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Hungarian forint weakens as inflation nears 40-year low\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief sees more rate cuts\par     * Zloty regains some losses in technical trade\par     * Government debt yield curves steepen, showing long-term\par uncertainty\par     By Karolina Slowikowska and Sandor Peto\par     WARSAW/BUDAPEST, Nov 12 (Reuters) - The forint eased on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Tuesday as Hungary's inflation fell to near 40-year lows, strengthening expectations that its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will cut interest rates even more, while other central European markets consolidated after falls. Government bond yield curves in the region, however, continued to steepen, indicating that worries over liquidity trends in global markets have not passed. Assets in the region fell on Monday in the aftermath of U.S. economic data that suggested the Federal Reserve may start to cut back its stimulus earlier than expected. Cheap funds from the Fed fuelled capital flows in emerging economies in the past two years, also allowing them to cut interest rates significantly to help their economies recover. Hungary said its headline inflation rate dropped to 0.9 percent in October from 1.4 percent in September, surprising analysts, whose consensus forecast was for no change. The forint weakened 0.3 percent against the euro by 1538 GMT, to 298.88. Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Gyorgy Matolcsy said the bank could further reduce its 3.4 percent base rate, which it has already more than halved since August last year, to counter falling inflation. "The rate cuts are not under threat from the inflation side, and the reductions slowly erode the forint's strength," one Budapest-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said. "Foreign markets are not very supportive now, but there is no scare either." Poland's zloty, meanwhile, rebounded after Monday's fall and was bid at 4.207, firmer by 0.2 percent. Long-term government bond yields edged up in both countries, indicating uncertainty over long-term trends. Poland's 10-year yield rose 7 basis points to 4.42 percent, while Hungary's corresponding yield rose 19 basis points to 6.08 percent. "The Polish market is reacting today to events on foreign markets, where after Friday's (U.S.) data there is pressure on prices," said Maciej Popiel, a fixed income trader at PKO \uc2 BP\uc2 . Hungarian 10-year yields have risen by about 70 basis points in two weeks, while expectations for rate cuts keep short-term yields near 3 percent. That steepening in the yield curve can continue, one Budapest-based fixed income trader said. "If U.S. data meet expectations, 10-year yields there could still continue to weaken, and then we (Hungarian long-term bonds) can also weaken further," the trader said. Elsewhere, the Czech crown was bid weaker by 0.1 percent against the euro at 27.012, still sticking to the 27 level which it eased to in a 5 percent plunge last week as the \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  started to sell it to help exports. \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief ruled out similar \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the Czech measure contributes to uncertainty in the region's markets, dealers said. Elsewhere in central Europe, Romania's leu was flat at 4.452 against the euro.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The region's equity markets were mostly flat after Monday's\par falls, but Prague's main index fell 0.7 percent.  \par                        CEE MARKETS SNAPSH