\par\par \b \uc2 Short-Dated Spanish, Italian Bonds Benefit From ECB Cut--Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,738 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2013\par 14:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1319 GMT [Dow Jones]--Two-year Spanish and Italian bonds are performing strongly after the ECB cut pushing yields sharply lower. Spanish 2-year bonds currently yield 0.97%, down around 12 bps from Wednesday's close, while comparable Italian bonds yield 1.26%, down 13 bps, according to data from Tradeweb. Market is now pricing in cheaper-for-longer money which adds fuel to the non-core carry trades. (nick.cawley@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1301 GMT The \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may keep the koruna weak and near the level of CZK 27 to the euro well into mid-2014, says Miroslav Frayer, an economist and strategist at \uc2 Komercni Banka\uc2  in Prague. "I suppose it will be till inflation starts increasing again," he says, adding that Komercni forecasts a negative Czech inflation rate in the first half of next year. "So if [this scenario] plays out, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  [will probably want] the koruna at such weak levels during the first half of next year," he says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  voted Thursday to launch \b \highlight22\uc2 FX interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost import prices while helping exporters. Other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , namely the Polish zloty and Hungarian forint, weakened on the news, but less dramatically than the koruna. (sean.carney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1300 GMT [Dow Jones] The euro takes a sharp hit across the board after the ECB surprised almost everyone with a 25bp cut to both the refi rate and marginal lending rate, while leaving the deposit rate at zero. EUR/USD slumped from around 1.3507 to a fresh 7-week low of 1.3354, although the rate announcement was delayed by one minute and the spot had started to dive beforehand. EUR/GBP dumped almost one penny to 0.8312, its lowest since January 2013. EUR/JPY dived to 131.90 from around 133.30, a fresh 1-month low for the cross. gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1258 GMT [Dow Jones]--Well, that's a major shock. To most of the market anyway. Dow Jones' survey showed only three economists betting on a cut today. Refi and Marginal rates cut by 25 basis points, but the discount rate is left unchanged at 0%. That's going to have a big signalling impact, but the fact is that it ain't going to change overall conditions in the euro zone by much. In a falling rate environment, it's the floor that counts. The deposit rate sets the floor, and the ECB has chosen not to experiment by taking it below zero. (geoffrey.smith@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1245 GMT [Dow Jones] Thursday's decision by the \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the koruna is a surprise to \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in light of the latest run of strong industrial and retail sales data. However, the bank says it does show that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very much afraid of deflation ahead; by weakening the koruna, that risk dissipates. BNPP's own model shows the pass-through from \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation to inflation would mean a EUR/CZK rate of 27.00, which the CB is now targeting and is some 5% higher than recently seen, and would add between 0.6 to 0.7 percentage points to headline CPI after 4 to 5 quarters, increasing the chances of the 2% target being met by early 2015. EUR/CZK now at 26.856 from around 25.77 prior the CB's reported \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (gary.tride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1238 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's drastic rate cuts and very low interest rate environment will push retail investments to funds and inspire more risk-taking, Concorde Fund Manager's Laszlo Szabo said. "No-one expected such a huge drop in the key policy rate. The decline in interest rates on deposits shocked retail clients," Mr. Szabo said. Between August 2012 and October 2013, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate setters cut the key policy rate from 7% down to 3.40%. Clients who stored their savings in bank deposits have already started channeling these to investment funds and short-term bond funds, but the fund manager recommends longer-term investments instead, such as absolute yield funds. Concorde has also witnessed a rise in capital inflow to its absolute return funds this year; investments in one of its funds jumped to 40 billion forints ($183 million) by the end of October from just HUF 15.5 bln at the beginning of this year. (veronika.gulas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 7:26 EST - FXCM's weaker-than-expected 3Q results should induce more profit-taking in shares of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  brokerage. Add on to its a 3% decline in retail volume last month and the 61% stock surge this year for FXCM--already 19% below September's high--and you have the making of a decent decline today. "Despite a muted trading environment, we are pleased to see the business still generating strong cash flow," says CEO Drew Niv. "Volatility declined throughout the quarter, resulting in lower trading activity, but our business is built to withstand these headwinds." With institutional activity more than tripling on year in 3Q, FXCM is making sure it's not just focused on one lever. Shares are inactive premarket. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:17 EST - Traders waiting on the ECB rate decision and the subsequent Draghi presser have kept EUR/USD basically in a 20pip range during London trading, while USD/JPY has been in an even-quieter 5pip range. The feeling among some traders is to ignore the euro but focus on euro crosses. Given central banks in Australia and the UK sitting tight, the play would be to sell the crosses, EUR/GBP in particular. That pair looks like it still has room to run on the downside at least until it finds support at the 100-month moving average of GBP0.7990. Traders expect the euro will underperform G20 counterparts as the year winds down. (vincent.cignarella@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1215 GMT [Dow Jones] Sterling is going nowhere after the BOE MPC delivered as expected with no change to the 0.5% key interest rate or the asset purchase total which it left at GBP374bln. GBP/USD trades unchanged at 1.6070, next resistance is at 1.6120, minor support at 1.6040. EUR/GBP trades at 0.8406, just a couple of ticks higher. Risk here now comes from the ECB rate decision at 1245 GMT, where the majority consensus is for unchanged rates, but the decision is seen less clear cut than the BOE after last week's sharp reported drop in euro zone inflation and record high jobless total. Next support for EUR/GBP is 0.8385, resistance at 0.8421. (gary.tride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1201 GMT [Dow Jones]--The Bank of England does as it was expected to, and, logically, really had to and leaves base rated alone - at record levels - yet again. Its asset purchase program was also unchanged. Surely this was so likely as to preclude any action in sterling markets: we may get more next week when the inflation report could shine new light on the bank's forward guidance. For now, it's eyes down for the ECB decision in about 45 minutes' time.(david.cottle@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0000 GMT [Dow Jones] The EUR/GBP charts indicate more weakness for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given the lethargic corrective recovery seen from Wednesday's five-week low at 0.8379. The sharp decline from the October 29 reaction high at 0.8584 signalled room for weakness to the key 0.8332/34 lows that formed a double-bottom base between September and early October. But last week's bearish outside week candle leaves the 0.8584 peak stranded, and Tuesday's break below 0.8442 issued the sell signal that offered additional downside risk to 0.8332/34. The body of congested resistance between 0.8450 and 0.8476 provides a solid barrier to the upside. EUR/GBP is at 0.8403.\par \par \uc2 -By Francis Bray, Dow Jones Chief Technical Analyst For Europe.\par \par \uc2 (francis.bray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 0000 GMT [Dow Jones] The rally from Thursday's marginal low at 141.05 in the December Bund future is targeting the highs of a bear pennant continuation pattern at 141.42 and 141.44 on the 60-minute chart, as risk of a significant bear trap has increased. The failure to extend the short-term bear wave to the 140.85 support during Thursday's morning session should concern bears, and a break above 141.44 would provide the additional boost needed to open single print resistance at 141.82 on last week's Market Profile chart. This in turn would bring the October 31 reaction high at 142.32 back within reach. A reversal below 141.05 would negate the bear trap threat, exposing 140.85. The December Bund future is at 141.24.\par \par \uc2 Market Profile is an intraday charting technique, seeking to evaluate market value as it develops during the day. The technique displays price on a vertical axis, and time/activity on a horizontal axis, creating bell curves that show normal and variable distributions in financial markets.\par \par \uc2 -By Francis Bray, Dow Jones Chief Technical Analyst For Europe.\par \par \uc2 (francis.bray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 07, 2013 08:19 ET (13:19 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020131107e9b7002bs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
