\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-Warsaw leads stocks up on U.S. debt deal hopes\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jason Hovet and Radu Marinas   \par \uc2 726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2013\par 15:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 PRAGUE/BUCHAREST, Oct 15 (Reuters) - Central European emerging stock markets, led by Warsaw, rose on Tuesday on optimism that a U.S. debt default will be averted, while the Polish zloty resisted softening pressures from lower-than-expected inflation.\par \par \uc2 There were signs in Washington that a Senate deal will be clinched to prevent a potentially calamitous default on federal debt.\par \par \uc2 Warsaw's stocks rose 1.49 percent to a nine-month high followed by Budapest with 0.97 percent and Prague . Telefonica rose 3 percent on the possibility Spanish parent Telefonica may sell a majority stake in it.\par \par \uc2 However, electricity producer CEZ fell 1 percent after its chief executive told Reuters the group was in talks with auditors on potential asset writedowns.\par \par \uc2 Poland's consumer price inflation edged down to 1.0 percent year-on-year in September from 1.1 percent in August, the statistics office said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by Reuters had expected last month's consumer prices to have risen 1.15 percent from a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Piotr Bujak\uc2  of \uc2 Nordea Bank Polska\uc2  said the main reason for the low inflation print was a deep drop in costs of education. "The underlying trend remains unchanged. Inflationary pressures are gradually gaining strength, but still subdued."\par \par \uc2 "The figures confirm rates to be kept on hold for long."\par \par \uc2 Polish inflation expectations are well-anchored, and monetary policy is likely to remain neutral, \uc2 Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Marek Belka said on Monday during a visit to Mexico.\par \par \uc2 By 1300 GMT the zloty was flat at 4.178 and was off a four-week high one day after dealers said state-owned bank BGK had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Both \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's forint and the Romanian leu edged up 0.2 percent while the Czech crown was a touch softer at 25.6 per euro.\par \par \uc2 Hungarian bonds were flat from late Monday levels but yields went down 4-5 basis points within the session, a trader said.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the Romanian markets shrugged off a failed sale of indebted rail freight carrier \uc2 CFR Marfa\uc2  that was required to complete an IMF-led aid deal earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Romania plans to relaunch the sale after a deadline to pay 170 million euros for a majority stake expired on Monday with no money entering state coffers.[ID: nL6N0I433P] Romanian company Grup Feroviar Roman (GFR) was the prospective buyer.\par \par \uc2 CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1510 CET\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ************************** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ************************\par Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par bid     close      change  in 2013\par Czech crown       25.600    25.560   -0.16%  -2.16%\par Hungarian forint  295.430   296.100   +0.23%  -1.51%\par Polish zloty       4.178     4.179   +0.02%  -2.49%\par Romanian leu       4.448     4.458   +0.24%  -0.10%\par Croatian kuna      7.615     7.615   +0.01%  -0.84%\par Serbian dinar    113.880   114.260   +0.33%  -1.39%\par Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1600 GMT\par **************************** STOCKS **************************\par Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par close      change  in 2013\par Prague                972.44    966.50   +0.61%  -6.38%\par Budapest           18712.52  18550.87   +0.87%  +2.97%\par Warsaw            2536.71   2498.77   +1.52%  -1.79%\par Bucharest          6009.41   5967.24   +0