\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-MOL hits 16-month low, crown tests  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  threat\b0\par\par\uc2 841 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2013\par 16:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOL shares down on Croatian INA stake deal worries * Czech Sept. 26 meeting minutes: \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  chances remain high \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Radu Marinas and Sandor Peto\par     BUCHAREST/BUDAPEST, Oct 4 (Reuters) - Shares in Hungarian\par oil and gas group MOL fell to a 16-month low on Friday\par as a spat between Budapest and Zagreb over its stake in Croatian\par peer INA escalated.\par     The Czech crown held near a four-month peak against the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 euro, testing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's threats to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while other Central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed. Stock markets in the region fell, including a 1.2 percent decline for MOL that led the Budapest bourse lower. Hungarian Prime Minister \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2  said on Friday that Croatia should consider buying MOL's stake in INA to prevent damage to bilateral relations. Relations between MOL and Croatia have been strained ever since MOL, which has a nearly 50 percent stake in INA, tried and failed to take majority control in early 2011. The row escalated this week after Croatia issued an \uc2 Interpol\uc2  and European arrest warrant for MOL chief \uc2 Zsolt Hernadi\uc2  in a bribery case. MOL has denied all the accusations. "It is weighed down by the continued uncertainty (over INA) that has been hanging over the shares for days," a Budapest-based equity dealer said. On \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the crown was a little volatile during the day, before steadying after minutes from the \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Sept. 26 meeting showed a majority of board members agreed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained likely. With interest rates at near zero, the bank has been mulling using this tool to ease monetary conditions further. But the board has voted against the measure at the last two meetings. The Czech economy is starting to recover from a record recession, although inflation has been running below-target all year and crown strength remains a concern. Weakening the crown would help prop up price growth by making imports more expensive, in theory encouraging people to spend now. But the measure could also potentially backfire by further depressing consumer demand. Dealers and analysts say the crown is testing levels that might prompt the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  into action. "The market might test how far it can push (the crown)," Raiffeisen said in a weekly note. "But given the current circumstances, i.e. still very fragile recovery and slowing inflation, significant crown appreciation would be exactly the case when most of the central bankers would decide to sell crown on the market, despite the uncertainties about the effect and future \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." Other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mixed, with Romania's leu firming 0.2 percent to the euro, while the Polish zloty was flat. The forint eased 0.2 percent to 297 in what one dealer said was a likely option expiry at midday. "If the dollar is weakish next week, the forint could firm, but it will stay between 295 and 300," the dealer said.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1531 CET\par  ************************** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par            