\par\par \b \uc2 DJ CEE Morning Briefing: Czechs May Launch  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean Carney   \par \uc2 843 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2013\par 03:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par Local/GMT \par 0930/0730 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Inflation Report \par 1300/1100 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  monetary policy decision \par 1430/1230 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  press briefing \par 1430/1230 Hungary Econ Minister Varga at conference \par 1500/1300 Hungary PM Orban at corporate event \par N/A Bulgarian parliament may hold no-confidence vote in govt \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monetary policy remains the central theme in central Europe Thursday, as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may vote to launch a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to loosen monetary conditions, hot on the heels of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slashing interest rates this week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 In Prague analysts expect no change to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark interest rate, which is at a historic low of 0.05%.\par \par \uc2 But if the monetary policy board deems it necessary to ease monetary conditions in the country that is emerging from recession, it will come via large-scale koruna sales.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is [now] the closest [it's been] to start \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a year of contemplation," said Miroslav Plojhar, economist and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Erste Bank.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Plojhar said its a 50-50 chance the bank votes for the move. However, the bank is not obliged to hold such a vote.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board hasn't found the need to launch an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet, but governor Miroslav Singer recently said a koruna-sale is likely while the country's inflation rate continues to undershoot the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2% target.\par \par \uc2 That comes as the \uc2 Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday cut its main rate to 3.60% from 3.80%, citing a slow economy and tame inflation as drivers.\par \par \uc2 In Sofia, Bulgaria's parliament has its last chance to hold a no-confidence vote in the left-leaning, Socialist Party-led coalition.\par \par \uc2 Opposition party Citizens for European Development of Bulgaria, or GERB, put forth the motion.\par \par \uc2 However, the party's lawmakers skipped a chance to hold the vote on Wednesday and it remains likely that GERB members will again fail to register in parliament for the vote Thursday, the last day the motion can be brought to a vote. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par EUR/CZK \par Latest 0150 GMT 25.832-94 \par Previous 2150 GMT 25.817-90 \par %Chg +0.04 \par  \par EUR/HUF \par Latest 0150 GMT 299.08-94 \par Previous 2150 GMT 299.40-300 \par % Chg -0.07 \par  \par EUR/PLN \par Latest 0150 GMT 4.2182-242 \par Previous 2150 GMT 4.2131-223 \par % Chg +0.08 \par  \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par Hungary \par          Wed    Tues \par 3 yrs    4.49%  4.52% \par 5 yrs    4.96%  4.98% \par 10yrs    5.72%  5.75% \par  \par Poland \par          Wed    Tues \par 3 yrs    3.19%  3.18%(1) \par 5 yrs    3.72%  3.66% \par 10yrs    4.35%  4.32% \par (1) 2 yrs \par  \par Czech Republic \par          Wed    Tues \par 3 yrs    0.39%  0.39% \par 5 yrs    1.11%  1.12% \par 10yrs    2.35%  2.35%(1) \par (1) 11 yrs \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG 20 \par 2,407.12 +14.06 +0.59% \par  \par  \par BUX \par 18,687.49 +298.17 +1.62% \par  \par  \par PX \par 960.25 -1.77 -0.18% \par  \par  \par OTHER NEWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Poland's economic recovery isn't under threat with most recent data confirming the belief that gradual expansion continues, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor Marek Belka said Wednesday night.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Belka added interest rates will remain unchanged until end of 2013 with no signs of inflation pressures in the economy, and the council will likely provide guidance early next year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Polish Airlines SA was forced to ground its \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boeing Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (BA) 787 Dreamliners again when it discovered that low-pressure fuel filters were missing in engines of two planes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The troubled flag-carrier blamed \f28 \uc2 \f2 Boeing\f28 \uc2 \f2  for the fault in a statement Wednesday. It said filters were missing in both engines of one aircraft and in one engine of another. It didn't say when the glitch was detected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: The Czech economy may shrink less than expected this year, and looks set to grow more than forecast in 2014 as a recovery at home and across Europe takes hold, the finance ministry said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In an unplanned update to its macroeconomic projections, the ministry now expects gross domestic product to contract 1.1% this year, less than its earlier forecast for a decline of 1.5%, and sees stronger growth of 1.3% next year, up from 0.8%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary's Export-Import Bank Zrt.'s Tuesday issuance of 400 million euros ($540 million) worth of bonds, took place at an interest level of 2.125%, Eximbank said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The issuance was guaranteed by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Multilateral Investment Guarantee Agency\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which is part of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  Group. The guarantee helped secure a triple-A rating for 95% of the bonds with rating firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Sean Carney at sean.carney@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Go to \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/}}}\uc2 \f2  for the WSJ blog on Central and Eastern Europe, covering business, politics, society and more, written by our correspondents across the region.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 25, 2013 21:57 ET (01:57 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130926e99q000b7\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Among Emerging Markets, Central Europe Offering Stability\b0\par\par\uc2 531 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2013\par 08:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Korean Newswires English Content\par \uc2 DJNKO\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL 9/3/13)\par \par \uc2 By Richard Barley\par \par \uc2 [Financial Analysis and Commentary]\par \par \uc2 Not all emerging markets are created equal.\par \par \uc2 Countries such as Turkey, Indonesia and India have seen their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  plummet as shifting expectations of U.S. monetary policy have sent hot-money flows into reverse. But the picture looks very different in Central Europe.\par \par \uc2 The Czech koruna strengthened in August, while the Hungarian forint and Polish zloty were only marginally weaker. Central European markets can't be entirely immune to global jitters about the Federal Reserve and conflict in Syria, but the region should continue to offer investors greater stability.\par \par \uc2 The big advantage for Central European countries is that they aren't dependent on the massive capital flows spurred by easy U.S. monetary policy, so don't fear their reversal.\par \par \uc2 While Turkey had a yawning current-account deficit of 6.5% of gross domestic product at the end of June, Hungary has swung to a surplus from a deficit of 7.2% of GDP in 2008. Poland has recorded its best current-account figures of the past decade in the past two months, Nomura notes.\par \par \uc2 On the growth front, Central Europe is accelerating -- albeit modestly and after extended weakness. The Czech Republic emerged from recession in the second quarter, growing 0.7% on the quarter, while Hungary's economy expanded 0.5% and Poland's 0.4%. Stronger growth in the euro zone -- particularly in Germany, a key trade partner -- should help the recovery continue.\par \par \uc2 Oddly, Central Europe may owe its current safe-haven status partially to the turmoil that has plagued Europe in recent years, as well as to European institutions much criticized for failing to prevent that crisis. The \uc2 European Union\uc2 's Excessive Deficit Procedure has forced countries to fix their fiscal problems.\par \par \uc2 Poland has a constitutional limit on debt set at 60% of GDP -- the Maastricht criteria for euro entry. Hungary has reduced its budget deficit to 1.9% of GDP in 2012 from 9.4% in 2006. The euro-zone crisis and bank deleveraging mean there has been no credit boom in Central Europe, unlike in Asia and Latin America.\par \par \uc2 There are still vulnerabilities. Czech politics are messy. In many other ways, though, the Czech Republic looks like the Central European equivalent of Switzerland -- its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is even thinking of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the government has pursued unorthodox fiscal policies including heavy windfall taxes on industry that may deter investment. The country is still grappling with the problem that large amounts of residential mortgages are denominated in Swiss francs, making a weaker forint a real problem.\par \par \uc2 In Poland, some worry about bond-market stability -- nonresidents owned 37% of domestic government bonds as of April -- and the government has seen its budget deficit for 2013 widen due to weak growth.\par \par \uc2 But if growth picks up in the euro zone, then Central Europe could offer investors opportunities. It may yet be a bumpy ride, but their stronger fundamentals should win out. [ September 02, 2013 18:46 ET (22:46 GMT) ] [ (END) Dow Jones Newswires ] [ 04-09-13 0610GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJNKO00020130904e9940000i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
