\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-Forint leads gains, region's bonds sell well\b0\par\par\uc2 833 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2013\par 16:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * U.S. Fed stimulus comments support emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Czech crown limited by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Romania sell bonds (Updates throughout)\par \par \uc2 BUCHAREST/WARSAW, July 11 (Reuters) - Most emerging European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed against the euro on Thursday after the U.S. Federal Reserve said it will stick to an easy monetary policy for now, while the prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the Czech crown weak.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the state debt management agency sold 47 billion forints worth of bonds, with yields around 60 basis points lower compared to yields accepted at the previous tender.\par \par \uc2 Romania also successfully placed bonds on Thursday with healthy demand supported by the comments from the Fed that its stimulus programme would continue for a while yet.\par \par \uc2 Hungary's June inflation inched up to an annual rate of 1.9 percent, still not far off a 38-year low hit earlier this year. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymakers have signalled they had room to cut interest rates further, a move priced in by markets.\par \par \uc2 "The bank's room for manoeuvre will continue to be defined by the external environment and the extremely loose monetary policy pursued by major central banks," said Erste Bank's Orsolya Nyeste.\par \par \uc2 "There is no particular obstacle to a July rate cut as external market conditions have turned for the better since last month."\par \par \uc2 Central and eastern European assets have plunged since May when the Fed signalled it would begin to taper its monetary stimulus that had sent a flood of money into emerging Europe. But Fed chief \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  said on Wednesday the stimulus would continue as inflation remained low and the unemployment rate high.\par \par \uc2 By 1416 GMT, the Hungarian forint was up 0.7 percent against the euro at 292.040. The Polish zloty and Romanian leu were each 0.2 percent up.\par \par \uc2 In Romania, where the leftist government is set to start talks next week on a new aid deal with the IMF and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has started cutting interest rates, traders said there was renewed interest for longer-dated debt on the secondary market.\par \par \uc2 The Czech crown was 0.2 percent weaker. With rates at a record low 0.05 percent, the \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to weaken the crown if it needs to ease monetary policy further.\par \par \uc2 "While (the crown) has strengthened in recent weeks (because of the EUR/USD), the space for further crown gains is limited because of threats of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," CSOB said.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in the region, Serbia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept its benchmark interest rate at 11 percent on Thursday, bucking market expectations for a third consecutive cut.\par \par \uc2 Serbia is among markets which have struggled recently as investors pull out on the view that the Fed will start withdrawing stimulus, making rate cuts in these countries riskier. The dinar was flat.\par \par \uc2 CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1616 CET\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ************************** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ************************\par Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par bid     close      change  in 2013\par C