\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-Stocks drop further,  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  catch breath\b0\par\par\uc2 717 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2013\par 17:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Warsaw stocks lead decline with 3.1 pct drop\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stabilises after sharp selloff on Wed\par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  seen cutting rates on Tuesday\par     * Serbian cbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stabilise dinar - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers\par     By Jason Hovet and Marcin Goettig\par     PRAGUE/WARSAW, June 21 (Reuters) - The sell-off in central\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 European stocks continued on Friday as investors cut their exposure to riskier markets after the U.S. Federal Reserve signalled a gradual end to its monetary stimulus programme, while \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds mostly stabilised. Poland's benchmark index led the losses, dropping over 3 percent as it was dragged lower by deep falls in local heavyweight stocks such as KGHM, Europe's second biggest copper miner, and coal miner JSW KGHM fell a further 7 percent on Friday to 123 zlotys, bringing its drop over the week to 22 percent after the stock suffered from a slide in copper prices and a lower than expected dividend. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly steady, but Hungary's forint remained under pressure ahead of an expected rate cut next week. The region's most liquid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Poland's zloty , shed 0.3 percent, holding around Wednesday's levels. The Czech crown eased 0.1 percent. Dealers said the Serbian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold about 20 million euros on Friday to stabilise the dinar. On Thursday the bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to sell about 30 million euros, dealers said. "Generally it is the Fed," said a Budapest-based dealer. "Positions are re-shaken, and that's normal. But people will continue to sit on cash and they will realize that they need to use it for something." Hungary's forint weakened by 0.7 percent, but held within Wednesday's range. \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to cut rates again on Tuesday but may express concerns that the emerging markets sell-off has hit forint assets and signal that the bank's easing cycle could end soon, a Reuters poll showed. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said the rate cuts could undermine the forint in the longer term, reducing the profits of investors holding the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Of course the lower carry can be a problem to the forint in the longer term," the dealer said. Bond yields stabilised in Poland, but continued to rise in Hungary after the jump the previous day triggered by the Federal Reserve news, which also drove central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  down as much as 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     \par                      CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1625 CET\par  ************************** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                              bid     close      change  in 2013\par  Czech crown                 25.833    25.808   -0.10%  -3.04%\par  Hungarian forint           299.000   297.030   -0.66%  -2.69%\par  Polish zloty                 4.332     4.318   -0.33%  -5.96%\par  Romanian leu                 4.526     4.520    -0.13%   -1.83%\par  Croatian kuna                7.484     7.488  +0.05% +0.90%\par  Serbian dinar              114.400   114.670   +0.24