\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 3-Foreign debt issue plan supports Hungary assets\b0\par\par\uc2 788 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2013\par 16:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Could be first Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue since 2011\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  shrug off inflation, verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 (Releads, adds detail on \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Poland inflation)\par \par \uc2 By Dagmara Leszkowicz and Sam Cage\par \par \uc2 WARSAW/BUCHAREST, Jan 15 (Reuters) - The prospect of a Hungarian foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt issue underpinned the country's assets on Tuesday and the forint and zloty shrugged off slowing inflation and efforts by officials to weaken \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Emerging European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly a touch higher despite inflation data that was bearish for Polish and Hungarian interest rates and a chorus of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at pressuring \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help export-driven economies.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2  mandated banks to arrange meetings with investors, which may lead to the first foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue of the junk-rated country since 2011, lending some support to the forint and local debt markets.\par \par \uc2 The forint rose 0.2 percent to 294.3 per euro by 1428 GMT and debt yields fell along the curve, with five-year paper trading at 5.75 percent.\par \par \uc2 "The government didn't say it in so many words but this will definitely be a new issue," one Budapest-based fixed income dealer said.\par \par \uc2 "We'll see what demand there will be but we can see there is both an opportunity and a need for an issuance here."\par \par \uc2 Lower inflation and the prospect of interest rate cuts have also pressured \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the zloty and forint have both lost about 1 percent in 2013 after hefty gains last year.\par \par \uc2 In Hungary, inflation slowed to a one-year low of 5.0 percent in December, below analysts' average forecast, and a central banker said it could be close to its 3 percent target in the next two years due to government cuts in household energy prices.\par \par \uc2 PAUSE IN POLISH CUTS\par \par \uc2 Poland's inflation eased below the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target for the first time in more than two years in December, strengthening the case for more interest rate cuts from the current 4 percent.\par \par \uc2 But last week's signal from \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it will pause its easing campaign to evaluate the state of the economy following three interest rate cuts is still underpinning the zloty and Polish bonds.\par \par \uc2 "We stick to our expectations of the main rate falling to 3 percent," said \uc2 ING Bank Slaski\uc2  economist Rafal Benecki. "The data is positive for the short end of the yield curve."\par \par \uc2 The zloty was a touch higher at 4.117 per euro. Elsewhere, the Czech crown lost 0.1 percent and Romania's leu was flat, though still close to a nine-month high hit last week due to the prospect of clinching a new \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  deal.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   --------------------------MARKET SNAPSHOT--------------------\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                     Latest   Previous Local    Local\par                                    close    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                             change   change\par          