\par\par \b \uc2 Hold RA CEE MARKETS 3-Leu highest since end-Aug as rally holds ground\b0\par\par\uc2 1,053 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Dezember 2012\par 16:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Leu rally slows, hits highest since Aug 31\par \par \uc2 * Chances of Romanian political limbo ending cheer London\par \par \uc2 * Other \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  little changed in illiquid trade\par \par \uc2 (Updates throughout)\par \par \uc2 By Jason Hovet and Dagmara Leszkowicz\par \par \uc2 PRAGUE/WARSAW, Dec 14 (Reuters) - The Romanian leu hit a 3-1/2-month high on Friday on rising hopes that an overwhelming victory for Prime Minister Victor Ponta in an election last weekend will stabilise policymaking.\par \par \uc2 Other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were little changed in a traditionally illiquid December trade, with investors abstaining from opening fresh positions ahead of the end of the year, traders say.\par \par \uc2 In Romania, Ponta has been locked in a bitter dispute with President Traian Basescu, who is reluctant to reappoint his leftist rival as prime minister. But he has limited options after Ponta's decisive win in Sunday's parliamentary poll.\par \par \uc2 With Basescu in Brussels for an EU summit, his absence is delaying talks on forming a new government, helping keep the country in limbo at a time when it is due to start negotiating a new \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  deal.\par \par \uc2 The leu gained almost 1 percent on Thursday thanks to the hopes that political uncertainty will ease. The rally slowed on Friday, with the unit up 0.04 percent to 4.474 per euro by 1425 GMT, after hitting its highest level since Aug. 31 at 4.468.\par \par \uc2 "The expectation is for political stability and a new IMF deal," said a Bucharest-based trader.\par \par \uc2 The leu's gains came as other emerging European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed weak on prospects of looser monetary policy to stimulate economies suffering under austerity at home and a slowdown in trade with the crisis-hit euro zone.\par \par \uc2 In Romania, a spike in inflation and the political uncertainty caused by the feud between its president and prime minister has prevented rate setters from cutting interest rates to help an economy on the edge of recession.\par \par \uc2 Ponta's party won a two-thirds majority in Sunday's election. The party tried unsuccessfully in the summer to remove Basescu from office, a move criticised by the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Basescu said at the time he would never again appoint Ponta as prime minister and he has given no clear signal since the election. Analysts say he may try to split Ponta's alliance of leftists, liberals and conservatives.\par \par \uc2 "More positive price action could come in the near term but the longer-term picture remains murky," ING analyst Vlad Muscalu said in a note.\par \par \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 , in a regular weekly emerging markets note, said it was more positive on the leu, the only unit in the region to have lost ground this year. The French bank said it had closed a long euro position against the leu.\par \par \uc2 RATE CUTS NEARER\par \par \uc2 The Polish zloty and Hungarian forint are among the best-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world this year with gains of around 9 and 11 percent, respectively.\par \par \uc2 But the two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are down up to 3 percent since a rally sparked by yield-chasers taking advantage of liquidity measures from global central banks started to fizzle in August, when expectations of easier monetary policy gained ground.\par \par \uc2 The forint, and Czech crown were mostly steady on Friday while Poland's zloty edged some 0.1 percent higher.\par \par \uc2 "The market is very illiquid now and a higher volatility - if such one appears - is not related with \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' fundamentals," said Piotr Kalisz, head of CEE Economics at \uc2 Citibank Handlowy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "But prospects of a sharper slowdown will probably push \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower as soon as the first quarter of next year and we expect the zloty to weaken to around 4.3," he added.\par \par \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meets next week, while Czech rate setters are also due to meet on policy. But with rates already near zero in the latter, markets are looking for signals on when they might opt for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a next easing tool.\par \par \uc2 "The CZK will underperform on the back of the weak growth outlook and risks of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Most are counting on \b \highlight22\uc2 Hungary\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBH) to continue an easing cycle by reducing its base rate a quarter of a percentage point to 5.75 percent.\par \par \uc2 "It seems apparent that the doves on the MPC are more or less in full control of monetary policy," \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 In Poland, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has started its easing cycle last month, cutting borrowing costs twice since then to 4.25 percent and the market widely expect another interest rate cut as soon as next month. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par --------------------------MARKET SNAPSHOT--------------------\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                     Latest   Previous Local    Local\par                                   close    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par                                            change   change\par                         