\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/Emerging Europe: Morning Briefing: Minutes from Hungary's Rate-Cut Meeting Due\b0\par\par\uc2 415 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2012\par 11:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Emerging Europe blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope}}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 By \uc2 Sean Carney\par \par \uc2 TODAY'S CALENDAR\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -- Local/GMT \par  \par    -- 1400/1200 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hungary \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  minutes \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungary's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wednesday will publish the minutes of its September rate-setting meeting at which the monetary policy board surprised markets and eased monetary policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ahead of the vote, analysts mostly expected no change to policy, but the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  made its second consecutive 25 basis-points interest-rate cut that brought the benchmark rate to 6.50%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rate setters have already revealed that the vote, just as the previous vote in August, passed by a four to three majority. In the previous vote, the newer, dovish members outvoted the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's governor and his two deputies who then opted to keep the rate unchanged.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The minutes can nonetheless provide further indication about the short-term outlook for monetary policy in Hungary and may show whether \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hungary\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will join the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in verbally \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on markets to ease recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OTHER NEWS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Polish and Slovak authorities Tuesday canceled their nation's respective bans on imports and sales of hard alcohol from the Czech Republic, according to news reports in both countries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary's right-wing Prime Minister Viktor Orban voiced concern Tuesday about what he said was increasing nationalism in neighbouring Romania, in comments set to raise tensions during Romanian elections in December.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ROMANIA: A Romanian High Court judge was sentenced Tuesday to four years in prison on corruption charges, a judicial sources said. Maria David was accused of receiving more than 200,000 euros ($258,000) in bribes in 2009 from a businessman found guilty of tax fraud. She promised to speak up for him at an appeals court.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BULGARIA: Support for Bulgaria's centre-right government and Prime Minister Boyko Borisov plunged between June and September, a new opinion poll showed Tuesday, nine months before elections are due. Forty-five percent of the 1,012 people polled by the Alpha Research Institute in September said they disapproved of the government, with only 18 percent still staunchly supporting it.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gergo Racz in Budapest contributed to this article.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sean Carney\f28 \uc2 \f2  at sean.carney@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 10-10-12 0907GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020121010e8aa000ht\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
